    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539212/casual-academic-talent-pool-business-istm": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Business - ISTM",
      "description": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Business - ISTM Apply now Job no: 539212 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Tutor, Lecturer, Other, Demonstrator Casual academic roles in 2026, School of Information Systems & Technology Management (SISTM) The School of Information Systems & Technology Management, UNSW Business School, is currently recruiting tutors for the following courses for 2026: Term 1 (T1) : COMM1190, COMM2822, COMM5007, INFS1602, INFS1603, INFS1701, INFS2602, INFS2603, INFS2604, INFS2609, INFS2631, INFS2701, INFS3603, INFS3604, INFS3700, INFS3959, INFS4777, INFS4831, INFS5602, INFS5603, INFS5700, INFS5704, INFS5720, INFS5730, INFS5777, INFS5831, INFS5871, INFS5907, INFS5917, INFS5959 Term 2 (T2) : COMM2822, COMM5007, INFS1603, INFS2604, INFS2608, INFS2701, INFS3300, INFS3604, INFS3605, INFS3700, INFS3703, INFS3822, INFS3873, INFS4854, INFS4929, INFS5604, INFS5700, INFS5705, INFS5710, INFS5720, INFS5848, INFS5731, INFS5929, INFS5978, INFS5997 Term 3 (T3) : COMM1190, COMM5007, INFS1602, INFS1701, INFS2602, INFS2603, INFS2604, INFS2702, INFS2822, INFS3605, INFS3701, INFS3703, INFS3830, INFS3922, INFS5602, INFS5631, INFS5700, INFS5704, INFS5710, INFS5720, INFS5730, INFS5885, INFS5888, INFS5917, INFS5922, INFS5997, INFS5978 Applicants: An expression of interest application is open to applicants who meet the essential tutoring criteria. Deadlines for submission: Term 1 2026 – December 5, 2025 Term 2 2026 – February 25, 2026 Term 3 2026 - June 2, 2026 Teaching into the UNDERGRADUATE PROGRAM A. Essential criteria: Current Information Systems students, including Honours, Combined Degree Students and Bachelor of Commerce students with an Information Systems Major, must meet the following criteria: Hold a qualification higher than that held by the students enrolled in the course (by the start of term of engagement) Have a WAM of 73 or higher BIS majors: Current BIS students may only apply for the PASS tutor role. (Must have completed BIS degree to apply as an undergraduate tutor.) BIS Honours: must have completed ALL levels 1-3 INFS courses Combined Degree Students: must have completed one degree and all ISTM courses B. Essential criteria: Applicants with graduate qualification (completed) To apply you will need to meet the following criteria: A relevant undergraduate/ postgraduate degree for teaching Information Systems & Technology Management courses Or Experience relative to qualifications, regard is had to teaching experience, experience in research, experience outside tertiary education, creative achievement, professional contributions and/or contributions to technical achievement to the relevant undergraduate course being taught. *No prior experience in tutoring is required *Must be available from Week 1 to end of Week 10 and for all class management, marking and associated duties *New tutors must be available to attend tutor training Teaching into the POSTGRADUATE PROGRAM A. Essential criteria: Applicants with graduate qualification (completed) To apply you will need to meet the following criteria: A relevant postgraduate degree for teaching Information Systems & Technology Management courses Or Experience relative to qualifications, regard is had to teaching experience, experience in research, experience outside tertiary education, creative achievement, professional contributions and/or contributions to technical achievement to the relevant postgraduate course being taught. *No prior experience in tutoring is required *Must be available from Week 1 to end of Week 10 and for all class management, marking and associated duties *New tutors must be available to attend tutor training Lodging your expression of interest New applicants, please submit the following documents: A cover letter outlining your interest in tutoring and the courses you are interested in. (note: PASS leaders do not need to have completed a PASS degree, however, applicants will need to have a DN or above in the course that they are applying for.) Any related teaching/tutoring/industry experience you may have A Curriculum Vitae which includes any work, teaching and student mentoring experience A recent transcript of your grades Submitting an application does not guarantee that any form of employment will be offered to you. As positions become available, suitable candidates will be contacted for an interview. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 01 Apr 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539214/casual-work-integrated-learning-wil-talent-pool-business-career-accelerator": {
      "title": "Casual Work Integrated Learning (WIL) Talent Pool - Business - Career Accelerator",
      "description": "Casual Work Integrated Learning (WIL) Talent Pool - Business - Career Accelerator Apply now Job no: 539214 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Tutor, Lecturer, Other, Demonstrator Make a difference to people’s lives Play a key part at UNSW Business School Multiple opportunities available Non-discipline specific opportunities UNSW Business School is seeking people with professional industry experience as well as academic teaching experience for several casual opportunities. These opportunities are designed to support our students to explore, develop and achieve their career aspirations in a way that aligns to each individual students’ career goals and personal interests.  Our courses enable students to put into practice broad business content, enabling students to develop relevant business skills. Work-Integrated Learning (WIL) is a crucial component of a UNSW Business degree. Through placements or project work, undergraduate and postgraduate students will gain valuable industry experience by examining organisational opportunities, problems, and issues through the lens of all our disciplines. To effectively deliver the face-to-face, hybrid, and online tutorials and workshops, we are looking for enthusiastic sessional/casual professionals who: Genuinely enjoy helping others progress through their life and learning journeys Have experience in coaching, mentoring, teaching, and/or learning facilitation Have professional industry experience, in commercial, government or for-purpose organisations for working with postgraduate students, a minimum of 10 years for undergraduate students a minimum of 5 years Bring a broad multi-disciplinary perspective to organisational issues and stakeholder engagement Thrive in collaborative environments to build solutions that create value Are motivated to complete job-related administrative tasks UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 01 Apr 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539222/casual-academic-talent-pool-medicine-health-school-of-clinical-medicine": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Medicine & Health - School of Clinical Medicine",
      "description": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Medicine & Health - School of Clinical Medicine Apply now Job no: 539222 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Tutor, Lecturer, Demonstrator The School of Clinical Medicine within the Faculty of Medicine & Health is seeking expressions of interest from qualified individuals for casual teaching opportunities in our postgraduate programs. We encourage Expressions of Interest from new and experienced tutors, demonstrators, markers and lecturers who are excited to be a part of an integrated teaching team. The EOI portal will allow you to select which courses you would like to teach and the extent of your qualifications and experience so we can better assess your suitability. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. About the role Your role is to provide casual teaching support to the course organisers. Your duties may include demonstrating in tutorials, workshops or practical classes, marking, and other miscellaneous course related activities. You will need: Excellent communication skills (both spoken and written formats) Ability to answer basic questions confidently and accurately and to provide specific constructive feedback to students and course convenors. Proficiency with software applications commonly used in teaching (e.g. Office 365: Word, Excel, Teams, and learning management systems such as Moodle). Commitment to follow the UNSW Code of Conduct which deals with confidentiality, discrimination, health and safety, harassment, etc Willingness to complete Workplace Health & Safety training and other relevant training as required by UNSW before commencing work. If you are applying for PSCY courses, you will need: Australian legal, psychiatric, psychological or other mental health qualifications and experience in custodial or court settings. If you are applying for SWCH courses, you will need: A postgraduate degree in Medicine, Nursing or Health/Medical Sciences, or another relevant discipline; Industry and/or teaching experience is highly desirable Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. #LI-DNI The School of Clinical Medicine within the Faculty of Medicine & Health is seeking expressions of interest from qualified individuals for casual teaching opportunities in our postgraduate programs. We encourage Expressions of Interest from new and experienced tutors, demonstrators, markers and lecturers who are excited to be a part of an integrated teaching team. Excellent communication skills (both spoken and written formats) Position Description Advertised: 01 Apr 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539319/casual-academic-talent-pool-business-agsm": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Business - AGSM",
      "description": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Business - AGSM Apply now Job no: 539319 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Tutor, Other, Demonstrator About AGSM We are looking for individuals who demonstrate a strong commitment to creating exceptional student experiences through high-quality, innovative teaching and learning practices. AGSM’s faculty community is comprised of highly qualified and experienced professionals.  If you possess a strong background in any of our key discipline areas, we look forward to hearing from you. Key disciplines Negotiations Skills Accounting and Financial Management Corporate Finance Data Analytics Entrepreneurship and Innovation Leadership Marketing Strategy Sustainable Business Technology Strategy and Management and Digital Innovation We offer more than 30 courses and 160 classes annually to our full-time and part-time MBA and Graduate Certificate students. Our classes are delivered face-to-face and online. About you To be successful, you will have: A PhD or Masters degree in a relevant discipline. Demonstrated track record of excellent teaching at MBA or post-experience levels. Excellent communication skills. Current industry engagement. High-profile thought leadership in your industry (preferred, but not essential). Please revert to the Position Description below for more details. Job Description - Casual Academic Engagements.pdf Additional Information Shortlisted candidates will be contacted to discuss their application and immediate or longer-term teaching opportunities. Contact the AGSM Academic team for any questions by emailing agsm@unsw.edu.au . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 01 Apr 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/540417/casual-academic-talent-pool-medicine-centre-for-big-data-research-in-health": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Medicine - Centre for Big Data Research in Health",
      "description": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Medicine - Centre for Big Data Research in Health Apply now Job no: 540417 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Tutor, Lecturer, Other, Demonstrator The Centre for Big Data Research in Health within the Faculty of Medicine & Health is seeking expressions of interest from qualified individuals for casual teaching opportunities in the Master of Science in Health Data Science program. We encourage Expressions of Interest from new and experienced tutors, demonstrators, markers and lecturers who are excited to be a part of an integrated teaching team. About the role Your role is to provide casual teaching support to the course convenors. Your duties may include demonstrating in tutorials, workshops or practical classes, marking, and other miscellaneous course related activities. You will need: A degree in statistics, biostatistics, epidemiology, data science, or a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Demonstrated strong interpersonal skills and ability to communicate efficiently with students and course convenors Proficiency with software applications commonly used in teaching (e.g. Office 365: Word, Excel, Teams, and learning management systems such as Moodle), as well as software packages (R, Python or SAS) Commitment to follow the UNSW Code of Conduct which deals with confidentiality, discrimination, health and safety, harassment, etc Willingness to complete Workplace Health & Safety training and other relevant training as required by UNSW before commencing work. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 01 Apr 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/532695/casual-talent-pool-language-teachers": {
      "title": "CASUAL TALENT POOL - Language Teachers",
      "description": "CASUAL TALENT POOL - Language Teachers Apply now Job no: 532695 Work type: Casual Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Knowledge Exchange One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best UNSW Canberra has been engaged by a large Federal Government client to manage and deliver high quality and innovative language services to officers preparing for overseas postings and for the maintenance of language skills of officers in Australia. We are seeking expressions of interest from candidates with experience in teaching the following Languages: Arabic Bislama Burmese Cantonese Farsi French German Hindi Indonesian Italian Japanese Khmer Korean Lao Malay Mandarin Mongolian Polish Portuguese Russian Solomon Islands Pijin Spanish Tagalog Tetum Thai Tok Pisin Turkish Vietnamese We are looking for local Canberra based tutors in the above languages, however open to interstate expressions of interest. You will only be contacted by the Language Services team should a position become available. Accountabilities Specific accountabilities for this role include: Face to Face training (1 hour Preparation provided for 4 hours of Delivery). Immersion courses. Lunchtime discussion groups. Exam conducting and marking. Report writing and other associated duties. Preparation of lesson plans, including teaching content and associated materials. Other duties within the scope of the classification, as requested. A person engaged is generally expected to have the following skills and experience: Qualification and subsequent experience relevant to the engagement. Proven ability and experience teaching a foreign language to English speaking students. A demonstrated understanding of teaching methods and techniques relevant to the engagement. Proven organisation and time management capability and experience. Proven experience managing competing priorities and meeting deadlines. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. For any inquiries, please contact Inma Martinez Espinosa via email at languageservices.cbr@UNSW.edu.au or phone 02 9348 2296. How to apply: Please apply via our online recruitment system. Please upload your CV. Position Description Advertised: 26 Mar 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/538059/jobs-on-campus": {
      "title": "Jobs on Campus",
      "description": "Jobs on Campus Apply now Job no: 538059 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Retail, Human Resources & Recruitment, Information Technology, Finance, Estate / Facilities Management, Student Services, Research Administration support, Library, Student Recruitment / Admissions, Marketing / Communications, Business Development / Industry Engagement, Fundraising / Philanthropy, Laboratory Technician, Engineer, Legal, Risk & Safety, Project Management, Strategy, Payroll, Procurement, Analyst, Governance, Policy & Compliance, Cyber, Entrepreneurship, Knowledge Exchange, Research Infrastructure, Management, Hospitality & Events, Clinical/Medical Research, Healthcare Jobs on Campus is a talent pool of current UNSW students who receive priority access to casual employment opportunities on campus. To join the talent pool and gain access to jobs ranging from paid ambassador roles to administrative and professional positions, students need to complete Employability training, to prepare them for the recruitment process at UNSW. The skills developed through applying for the Jobs on Campus talent pool are highly transferable and can be used in other casual and professional job application processes. Please note : This is a pilot program, so there may be occasional issues. If you experience any problems or have questions, please contact the Employability team at employability@unsw.edu.au . Position Description Advertised: 24 Feb 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 31 Dec 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/537874/casual-professional-talent-pool-administrator-unsw-canberra": {
      "title": "Casual Professional Talent Pool - Administrator - UNSW Canberra",
      "description": "Casual Professional Talent Pool - Administrator - UNSW Canberra Apply now Job no: 537874 Work type: Casual Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Administration UNSW Canberra is seeking Expressions of Interest from suitably experienced candidates for casual Admin Support roles across Levels 4–6. This EOI will be used to establish a casual pool to provide proactive, effective administrative support to meet operational and service delivery needs. Appointments will be made at a level in line with the successful applicant’s skills, experience, and the complexity of duties assigned. Role Under the direction of the Defence Security Manager, the Administrator will support the implementation of UNSW security system processes across all UNSW business areas, in alignment with the requirements of the Defence Industry Security Program (DISP). This role offers opportunities for training and development, with the potential for progression to higher-level roles and consideration for ongoing employment, subject to performance and business requirements. Key Responsibilities (Level 5) Depending on the level of appointment, responsibilities may include: Providing high-quality, day-to-day administrative and operational support Offering advice and support on administrative processes and procedures Developing and maintaining filing systems, spreadsheets, databases, and websites Preparing agendas, minutes, correspondence, briefing notes, and guidelines for meetings and committees Responding to enquiries from internal and external stakeholders, escalating where appropriate Facilitating administrative requests including purchasing, travel, credit card reconciliation, expense reimbursements, payment requests, and billing processes Supporting projects, coordination activities, and service improvement initiatives Complying with all University health and safety policies and procedures About You Relevant qualifications and/or substantial administrative experience Strong organisational skills and attention to detail High-level written and verbal communication skills Proficiency in Microsoft Office and administrative systems The ability to manage competing priorities and work both independently and collaboratively Eligibility The successful candidate will be required to undergo pre-employment checks prior to commencement in this role. These checks include verification of Australian citizenship, identity, a criminal history check, and two reference checks. No additional documentation or information will be required unless specifically requested by UNSW. To be eligible for this position, you must be an Australian citizen in order to meet AGSVA eligibility requirements for a Baseline security clearance. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Please submit your application by clicking Apply Now. To be considered, your application must include: A current CV A cover letter addressing the skills and experience outlined in the Duty Statement #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 06 Feb 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/536864/casual-academic-talent-pool-faculty-of-law-and-justice": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Faculty of Law and Justice",
      "description": "Casual Academic Talent Pool - Faculty of Law and Justice Apply now Job no: 536864 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Tutor, Lecturer, Other, Demonstrator The UNSW faculty of Law & Justice includes: School of Private and Commercial Law School of Global and Public Law School of Law, Society and Criminology Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Tutor, Lecturer, Other, Demonstrator To register your interest in joining the Casual Academic Talent Pool for UNSW Law and Justice, please fill in the below Expression of Interest form. You can view details for specific courses listed in this form by searching in the UNSW Course Outline Finder . Expressions of Interest  |  2027 Teaching Preferences Who we are UNSW Law & Justice is a top-ranking faculty and a global leader in law and criminology education and research, setting the pace in Australia for over 50 years. The School of Global & Public Law draws on the faculty’s strengths in international and comparative law and the two main branches of Australian public law – constitutional and administrative law.  In everything we do, we seek to combine sound analytic frameworks with practical approaches that make a meaningful difference to the world. The School of Private and Commercial Law is home to world-leading experts in the areas of law that govern our commercial dealings with and obligations to each other. In our teaching, research and policy-oriented work, we seek to engage critically with local and international laws. We’re committed to producing well-rounded, work-ready graduates who have an exceptional understanding of private and commercial law. UNSW School of Law, Society and Criminology emphasises the social relevance and impact of rigorous research and teaching. We’re socially engaged interdisciplinary academics and students who share a commitment to driving positive change in law, criminology and justice for the benefit of society. We collaborate with leading researchers, teachers and professionals around the world. About the role UNSW Law & Justice is seeking expressions of interest from qualified individuals for casual teaching opportunities in 2027 courses across all schools. Our academics are engaged in teaching across the LLB, BCCJ, graduate JD and LLM programs, but our students are also exposed to diverse methods of teaching and learning from our casual Teaching Fellows, who include leading judges, legal practitioners and policy officers. Teaching Fellows are an important and valued part of teaching delivery at UNSW Law & Justice. We are looking for professionals conversant with and passionate about the law to join our experienced Teaching Fellow community. We are particularly looking for people with an interest in participatory teaching methods and inculcating in students an appreciation of the connection between law and justice. About you For LLB, JD, and LLM courses, you will need: A Masters or PhD degree in law or another relevant discipline; or A Bachelor’s degree in law or another relevant discipline plus at least 2 years of in-depth professional and/or educational experience that demonstrates advanced legal knowledge, independent learning, critical thinking and problem solving and communication and interaction skills. For BCCJ courses, you will need: A Masters or PhD degree in criminology, law, or another relevant discipline; or A Bachelor’s degree in criminology, law, or another relevant discipline plus at least 2 years of in-depth professional and/or educational experience that demonstrates advanced knowledge, independent learning, critical thinking and problem solving and communication and interaction skills. Additional Information Teaching vacancies vary from Term to Term, depending on availability of permanent staff, course offerings and student enrolments. Employment is therefore offered on a Term basis. All employment details, including the duties required, duration of employment and relevant pay rates, will be confirmed with the successful applicant(s) prior to the commencement of duty. Offers of employment will be made in accordance with the UNSW (Academic Staff) Enterprise Agreement 2023. Closing Dates: Recruitment process will commence approximately 10 weeks prior to the start of the term. Term dates 2027 (approximate, exact dates TBD) Summer Term (11 Jan – 7 Feb) Term 1 (8 Feb – 23 May) Term 2 (24 May – 12 September) Term 3 (13 September – 20 December) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 03 Dec 2025 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/531035/casual-talent-pool-liaison-librarian": {
      "title": "CASUAL TALENT POOL - Liaison Librarian",
      "description": "CASUAL TALENT POOL - Liaison Librarian Apply now Job no: 531035 Work type: Casual Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Library Expression of interest We are seeking expressions of interest from qualified candidates with demonstrated experience in academic libraries (preferable), or strong transferable skills, for casual/fixed term employment in the UNSW Canberra Liaison Librarian team. About the Role: Role: Liaison Librarian Salary: HEW 7 Location: UNSW Canberra, ADFA campus Requisite qualifications: Degree/postgraduate degree in an information management related discipline. Accountabilities: Provide high quality information, reference and research services, including specialised consultations to the UNSW Canberra university community Advise and support teaching academics on the selection and use of information resources for learning, including course readings guidance Produce and update digital teaching and learning objects (DTLO) including creating and maintaining subject/information guides and online tutorials In collaboration with colleagues across the Library, undertake collection development activities in a discipline area As a discipline specialist, act as the Library’s representative for a School/s and/or research centre/s and deliver comprehensive general and specialised information literacy and client education activities to UNSW Canberra stakeholders Other duties within the scope of the classification as required. About you To be successful you will have: A degree or postgraduate qualification in an information management related discipline with subsequent relevant experience (at least 3 years), preferably in an academic library). Eligibility for Associate membership of the Australian Library and Information Association (ALIA) will be well regarded. Demonstrated experience in developing and delivering instructional resources and information literacy training sessions Well-developed liaison, interpersonal, communication, and presentation skills, together with a demonstrated ability to build and maintain effective working relationships with stakeholders Sound understanding of the higher education research lifecycle, including research impact and analytics, scholarly communication and publishing, research data management and open access Strong attention to detail and organisational skills Excellent experience in Microsoft Office software and other applications/databases. If this sounds like you, please submit your resume and a summary of your skills and experience to support your suitability for this role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. For any questions regarding this opportunity, contact Semra Griffiths via email at semra.griffiths@unsw.edu.au or phone 02 5114 5413. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 13 Mar 2025 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/505076/casual-audiovisual-production-roles": {
      "title": "Casual Audio-Visual Production Roles",
      "description": "Casual Audio-Visual Production Roles Apply now Job no: 505076 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Hospitality & Events Audio Visual Production Staff Employment Type: Casual Location: Kensington, NSW About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged, and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Venues & Events are specialists in delivering meetings and events for internal and external clients across a variety of venues, including the UNSW CBD Campus, John Niland Scientia Building, Clancy Auditorium, Science Theatre. Venues & Events coordinate and deliver the venue logistics and audio-visual support for meetings and events at UNSW. Why Your Role Matters: The audio-visual team are responsible for providing superior customer services and delivering AV services to internal and external clients. You will work on concerts, conferences, eisteddfods, gala dinners, video production and more as well as supporting core University activities on campus. UNSW Venues & Events are committed to providing the best production services possible. Some of the AV equipment and software you will work with includes TriCaster and BlackMagic video switchers, Allen & Heath audio mixers, Sony 4K HDR cameras, Panasonic PTZ cameras, and Hog 4 lighting consoles. You will be responsible for supporting event production and technical requirements for a broad range of events Who You Are: You have minimum 2 years’ experience working within the audio-visual industry, as well as: Operating Audio, Vision and Lighting live events. Delivering an excellent customer experience. Experience with live streaming virtual and hybrid events. Experience with NDI and Dante technologies. Camera operating experience desired but not essential. Please refer to the Position Description for this role by clicking on “Position Description” below before applying. You must have working rights in Australia and have an Australian bank account. Overseas applications cannot be considered. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: - National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. To Apply: To be considered for the above positions please upload your CV and respond to the questions in the application form before clicking submit. You must submit your application online, we cannot accept emailed applications. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2024 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/522394/talent-pool-casual-professional-bus-ca-mba-career-coach": {
      "title": "TALENT POOL - Casual Professional - BUS (CA) - MBA Career Coach",
      "description": "TALENT POOL - Casual Professional - BUS (CA) - MBA Career Coach Apply now Job no: 522394 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Services The Career Accelerator is seeking expressions of interest for casual opportunities for MBA Career Coaches to work with highly experienced MBA students. Contribute to transformative career journeys and join one of Australia’s leading research and teaching universities. The Career Accelerator has a dedicated team of career coaches that provide tailored support to AGSM students, helping and guiding them throughout their degree to achieve their career goals. We are looking for experienced professionals who have: Proven expertise in coaching workshop design and facilitation (coaching qualifications preferred). A deep understanding of the Australian employment market. Experience and/or qualifications in career development. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Availability for Face-to-Face and online appointments. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Please note that you must have working rights in Australia. Closing dates: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 19 Jan 2024 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/534991/lecturer-senior-lecturer-school-of-information-systems-technology-management-unsw-business-school": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - School of Information Systems & Technology Management - UNSW Business School",
      "description": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - School of Information Systems & Technology Management - UNSW Business School Apply now Job no: 534991 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Lecturer, Lecturer Employment Type: Full time Duration: Tenure Track position Remuneration: Level B from $133,065 + 17% superannuation and leave loading and Level C from $161,619 + 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW Australia About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The School of Information Systems & Technology Management (SISTM) is one of eight schools within the Business School that comprises over 30 staff from all over the world. It offers a vibrant and dynamic academic community engaged in leading-edge research, innovative teaching as well as constructive community and professional engagement. SISTM is one of the largest and oldest such schools in Australia. The School is currently ranked 1st in Australia, 2nd in Asia, and 15th worldwide in the latest Association of Information Systems (AIS) research productivity ranking and has highly developed local and international links. Why Your Role Matters: The School of Information Systems & Technology Management at UNSW Business School is hiring for two tenure-track positions, at either the level of Lecturer (Level B) or Senior Lecturer (Level C), in the area of Information Systems, Business Analytics, or Operations Management. A Lecturer (Level B) is expected to conduct scholarly research and professional activities, both nationally and internationally, and make substantive contributions in the areas of teaching, engagement and leadership in line with the expectations of the School and Faculty. A Senior Lecturer (Level C) will additionally be expected to conduct high-quality research that is recognised internationally and makes significant contributions to educational leadership and excellence in the School and Faculty. The role reports to the Head of School and has no direct reports. The level of appointment will be commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications and experience. If required, visa sponsorship and relocation support will be considered for the successful candidate. Who You Are: A PhD in Information Systems, Business Analytics, Operations Management, or a closely related business field by the end of 2026. Demonstrated excellence in publishing in leading IS or OM conferences and journals (e.g., UTD24, Financial Times 50, AIS Basket of 8). Experience in teaching technical (AI, database, networking) or business analytics courses is highly desirable, as is experience with UX design. Proven commitment to staying current with discipline knowledge and developments. Experience in teaching and learning design, development and delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate levels. Experience with educational technologies, online delivery methods, and industry engagement. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. To learn more about this position and view the full list of criteria, a copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Excellent career development opportunities Access to an academic salary supplementation scheme Up to 17% superannuation contributions and leave loading payments Flexible working arrangements (work from home, flexible hours, etc.) Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Complete a 1–2-page cover letter addressing how you meet the requirements of this position and submit your application online by Friday November 6 th at 11:30 pm Sydney Australia time. Get in Touch: There will be select opportunities for informal meeting about the position at INFORMS Annual Meeting in San Francisco. If you are interested in being considered for a meeting, or If you have any questions regarding the recruitment process or role please contact Amy Denney, Talent Acquisition Partner at a.denney@unsw.edu.au . Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542968/lecturer-in-energy-systems": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Energy Systems",
      "description": "Lecturer in Energy Systems Apply now Job no: 542968 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer The Opportunity The School of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications in the Faculty of Engineering at UNSW is seeking a Lecturer for a teaching and research role in Electrical Energy Systems and Power Engineering. This role will help strengthen the School’s capabilities in power systems, high-voltage systems, system modelling, simulation and control, while supporting interdisciplinary research across telecommunications, control systems, signal processing and microelectronic systems. Alongside delivering high-quality teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate Electrical Engineering programs, you will supervise Honours and postgraduate project students and contribute to course administration and academic quality assurance. With established strengths across electrical engineering, telecommunications, signal processing, communications, energy systems, robotics and emerging technologies, UNSW offers an exceptional environment for academics seeking to advance their research profile, contribute to interdisciplinary initiatives and deliver outstanding teaching. You will undertake research that advances electrical energy systems and power engineering, engage with industry, government and interdisciplinary collaborators, and contribute to innovation, knowledge exchange and the development of the next generation of engineers and researchers. Position Details Salary : Lecturer (Level B)- AUD $133,065 to $156,864 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full-time Appointment type: Fixed term, 2-year convertible tenure track (Fixed-term appointment with the potential to convert to continuing employment, subject to a successful review.) Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia Reporting line: This role reports to the Head of School and has no direct reports. About You You are an academic with expertise in power systems or electrical energy engineering, with the ability to contribute to high-quality teaching, research and scholarly activity across Electrical Engineering. You are committed to delivering engaging learning experiences and supporting positive outcomes for students across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. You will bring experience in teaching and learning design, curriculum development and delivery, together with the ability to use educational technologies and online learning methods. Success in this role requires a collaborative and adaptable approach, with highly developed communication, interpersonal and organisational skills. You will have a demonstrated track record of high-quality and high-impact research in power systems, together with the capacity for potential research leadership. You will be committed to supporting students from diverse backgrounds, contributing to equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives, building effective relationships across disciplines, and engaging with industry and professional networks. Experience with industry or work-integrated learning will be highly regarded. About UNSW UNSW is a world‑leading institution recognised for its scale, prestige, and impact. With strong industry engagement and partnerships across sectors, UNSW provides a unique environment where academic expertise translates into real‑world outcomes. The university is home to cutting‑edge research that drives innovation and societal progress, while its excellence in teaching ensures students are prepared to lead in their fields. For academics, UNSW offers an outstanding platform to flourish — combining world‑class facilities, collaborative networks, and a culture of innovation that supports both career growth and meaningful contributions to the wider community. The UNSW School of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications is one of Australia's largest and most prestigious schools of its kind. Home to more than 1,500 students and over 40 academic staff, the School is internationally recognised for excellence in teaching, research, and industry engagement across electrical engineering, energy systems, telecommunications, signal processing, control systems, microelectronics, quantum engineering, and emerging technologies. For further information about our School, please visit - https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/our-schools/electrical-engineering-telecommunications Skills and Experience A PhD in Electrical Engineering or a related discipline, along with relevant work experience. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated experience and evidence in teaching and learning design, development and/or delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate levels in the area of power systems or electrical energy engineering, as well as across the broad field of Electrical Engineering. Demonstrated track record in power systems research, along with outcomes of high quality and high impact. Demonstrated evidence of research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. Experience using and/or designing with educational technologies and online delivery methods. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence (e.g. relevant discipline based curriculum design and development at a variety of levels and scales). Demonstrated knowledge of industry or experience of work-integrated learning (desirable). Evidence of ability to support and inspire students from diverse backgrounds and support student equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives. A track record of significant involvement with the profession and/or industry. High level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students and staff. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for this position can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply: Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV, cover letter and responses to the Skills and Experience. If you have had any interruption in your career, you are encouraged to include a ROPE statement as part of your application. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: For role-specific inquiries, please contact Prof Jinhong Yuan (Head of School) E: j.yuan@unsw.edu.au For questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Eugene Aves (Talent Acquisition Consultant) E: eugene.aves@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:50 pm (Sydney time) on Tuesday, 27 October 2026 As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options, including working from home, for eligible staff. UNSW provides 26 weeks of fully paid maternity or primary carers leave upon commencement of employment (subject to eligibility). UNSW is committed to enhancing the experience of ECAs by providing a supportive and collegial work environment and a dedicated framework to deliver support services to assist their research, teaching, and career development. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542814/associate-dean-indigenous-education-student-success-identified-unsw-science": {
      "title": "Associate Dean, Indigenous Education & Student Success (Identified) - UNSW Science",
      "description": "Associate Dean, Indigenous Education & Student Success (Identified) - UNSW Science Apply now Job no: 542814 Work type: Full Time, Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Academic Leadership, Senior Lecturer, Professor, Associate Professor, Lecturer, Indigenous Employment Type/Duration: Full time, flexible - the leadership appointment will be fractional between (2.5 days to 4 days). A continuing academic role will sit alongside the leadership role so the appointee remains active in teaching /and or research Remuneration: $150,148 - $167,834 (Level C), $179,630 - $191,415 (Level D), Level E ($247,295) or competitive salary package (Associate Dean) full-time equivalent plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW, with hybrid working arrangements This is an identified position, and applicants must be Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. Confirmation of Aboriginality or Torres Strait Islander status will be required. UNSW has obtained an exemption under Section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people only. Why this role matters As we continue to grow our Indigenous workforce and strengthen Indigenous leadership across the University, we are seeking an outstanding Indigenous professional or academic to join the Faculty of Science. At UNSW, we're committed to creating meaningful opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and building a workforce that reflects the communities we collaborate with. We recognise the importance of Indigenous knowledges, leadership, and lived experience, and we're seeking an Indigenous academic leader to help shape the future of our Faculty of Science. Whether your background is in education, student success, Indigenous engagement, leadership, professional practice, research, or academia, we encourage you to explore this opportunity. We welcome candidates who bring demonstrated Indigenous leadership, cultural knowledge, lived experience, community connection, student engagement expertise, curriculum development experience, or broader professional expertise. More about the Associate Dean role As Associate Dean Indigenous Education and Student Success, you will shape Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander education, student success and curriculum across the Faculty of Science. You will lead the representation of Indigenous Knowledges across the Faculty curriculum, working with colleagues, students and communities to strengthen teaching, learning and assessment centred on Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing, being and doing. The role will include teaching responsibilities, and the appointee will be supported to actively engage in these. You will shape Indigenous student success, engagement and educational experiences, while advancing innovative approaches that authentically embed Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representation. As a key advisor, you will embed Indigenous Knowledges and Knowledge Systems across the Faculty’s academic work and contribute to meaningful educational transformation. You will collaborate with Indigenous staff across the University to strengthen shared leadership and collective impact. As a member of the Faculty’s senior leadership team, you will report to the Deputy Dean Education. Whilst there isn’t any direct report now, there may be one in the future. You can find the full details of the responsibilities in the position description. What’s on offer The Associate Dean position will be appointed for a term of up to two years, with the possibility of renewal for a second term. The leadership appointment will be fractional, between 0.5 FTE and 0.8 FTE, with the final fraction agreed with the successful applicant. The successful applicant will also hold a continuing academic appointment within the Faculty , providing an ongoing substantive academic position beyond their term as Associate Dean. During the Associate Dean appointment, they will remain active in teaching and/or research, with the balance of leadership and academic responsibilities agreed as part of workload negotiation. Depending on your qualifications, skills, experience, and career aspirations, appointment may be made at Senior Lecturer (Level C), Associate Professor (Level D), or Professor (Level E) level. Who you are We are seeking candidates who can demonstrate some combination of: Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander identity, cultural knowledge, lived experience, and community connection Experience or capability in teaching, curriculum development, educational leadership, or professional practice Leadership experience in higher education, education, community, government, industry, not-for-profit, or related sectors A passion for advancing Indigenous Knowledges, student success, engagement, and participation Experience developing programs, initiatives, curriculum, partnerships, or community-led outcomes Strong relationship-building, consultation, stakeholder engagement, and influencing skills Strategic thinking and the ability to translate vision into meaningful outcomes A PhD, Masters by Research, ideally related to Science, Education or equivalent professional, leadership, community, and/or lived experience is welcomed Candidates with a Science background will be highly regarded. If this opportunity interests you, but your experience does not align perfectly with the above, we still encourage you to apply. How to Apply: please submit your resume as well as a brief statement outlining your interest in the role and how your experience, leadership, cultural knowledge, and/or lived experience aligns with the opportunity Applications close: 21 October 2026 at 11.55PM In addition to creating a safe and inclusive workplace for our Indigenous staff, we offer other great staff benefits, such career development opportunities, a generous superannuation, 10 days cultural leave paid per year, additional leave over the end of the year, and other discounts. You can find more information here. To find out more about how we support our Indigenous staff and grow their careers, refer to this page Careers at UNSW for Indigenous Australians | UNSW Sydney To access our Indigenous Strategy, refer to https://www.indigenous.unsw.edu.au/ Any questions? For any questions about the recruitment process, if you need an adjustment or to submit your application in an alternative format, contact your recruiter, Anne de Voronine at a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au For any questions about the kind of support available for our Indigenous staff, get in touch with our Manager, Indigenous Employment, Nat Chenhall n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander status will be required. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543258/senior-lead-decision-modelling-and-advisory": {
      "title": "Senior Lead, Decision Modelling and Advisory",
      "description": "Senior Lead, Decision Modelling and Advisory Apply now Job no: 543258 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Analyst Employment Type: Continuing, Full-time, 35 hours per week opportunity Remuneration: Level 9 – from $153,933 plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid working conditions) About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. Why your role matters UNSW Planning and Performance (UPP) builds capability in business intelligence, student load forecasting, and data-driven performance as a trusted strategic partner to university leaders. Moving beyond traditional KPI reporting, the Senior Lead, Decision Modelling and Advisory drives the design, governance, and evolution of institutional performance frameworks and end-to-end modelling capabilities. This role brings together multi-system quantitative and qualitative data, transposing it into well-structured decision-support tools and practical, action-oriented insights to inform strategic planning and operational trade-offs across the institution. Working closely with executive, academic, and professional stakeholders, the position combines strong technical modelling with an advisory focus to influence executive decision-making. Reporting to the Head, Planning and Performance (with 0–2 direct reports), the ideal candidate combines expertise in quantitative methods (finance, analytics, consultancy) with a business partnering background and an innovative mindset, challenging established ways of working to continuously improve processes, benchmarking, and the university's overall strategic performance. About the Role The successful applicant will be required to undertake and have experience in the following areas of expertise: Lead the design, development and continuous improvement of institutional models, including product viability, forecasting, statistical analysis and scenario planning. Oversee University-wide resource allocation and performance frameworks, establishing clear methodologies, measures, targets and decision-making principles. Monitor and refine University KPIs and performance metrics, leveraging strategic priorities, sector trends and benchmarking to evaluate progress and risks. Provide evidence-based advisory support to senior leaders on competitive positioning, trade-offs and growth opportunities to drive institutional performance. Translate complex quantitative and qualitative data into clear executive insights, reporting and practical decision-support materials. Build trusted partnerships with academic and professional stakeholders to support the effective adoption of models, frameworks and analytical practices. Establish quality assurance standards and governance processes to ensure the integrity of models, while upskilling team and university-wide decision-support capabilities. About You Substantial experience designing, developing and governing complex institutional models, forecasting frameworks and resource allocation methodologies within large, complex organisations. Highly developed analytical, problem-solving and critical thinking skills, with a proven ability to structure ambiguous qualitative and quantitative data into clear, actionable executive insights. Exceptional communication and stakeholder management capabilities, with demonstrated success in influencing senior leaders, driving strategic outcomes and building analytical capability across teams. Advanced technical expertise in data analysis and modelling using tools such as Databricks, Excel, Power Pivot and Power BI, alongside working knowledge of SQL, Python or DAX. Proven project management experience managing concurrent initiatives, re-engineering complex systems and processes, backed by relevant tertiary qualifications in analytics, business, economics or a related discipline. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the Position Description ) before Sunday 18 th October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/538445/postdoctoral-fellow-indigenous-targeted-cities-institute": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow (Indigenous Targeted) - Cities Institute",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow (Indigenous Targeted) - Cities Institute Apply now Job no: 538445 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate, Indigenous Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term contract January 2027 until March 2029 (negotiable) Remuneration: Level B $133,065 - $156,864 + 17% Super + Leave Loading Location: Kensington, NSW Benefits: Cultural Leave and Indigenous Employee Network Whilst this role is open to everyone, we strongly encourage Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples to apply. Why Your Role Matters The Postdoctoral Fellow (Level B) will undertake independent and collaborative research within UNSW Cities Institute , as part of an Australian Research Council Linkage project, contributing to high-quality academic and policy outputs. The project focuses on the intersections between Indigenous planning, land use and land development, and state planning. The role requires close and effective engagement with Aboriginal partners, and a range of non-Indigenous partners, and close alignment with ethical research frameworks. The position reports to the Scientia Fellow and has no direct reports. Specific accountabilities include: Engage in individual and collaborative research consistent with disciplinary practice Undertake literature and policy reviews and develop expertise in Indigenous planning and land use and development Undertake independent fieldwork, including qualitative interviews with Aboriginal partners. The position requires travel and several weeks of fieldwork in different towns across NSW Produce high-quality research outputs that contribute to disciplinary knowledge Conduct research independently under limited supervision Develop a research profile and contribute to competitive grant applications Contribute to project management and the timely delivery of academic and policy outputs Adhere to AIATSIS Code of Ethics and Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property protocols About You: A PhD in a relevant discipline, e.g. geography, planning, political economy, law (expertise in planning and geography is highly desirable) Extensive experience working with Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander communities on collaborative research Experience undertaking research under the AIATSIS Code of Ethics for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Research Understanding of Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP) Demonstrated commitment to Indigenous self-determination and community wellbeing Demonstrated research experience including qualitative data collection, policy analysis, and fieldwork Knowledge of qualitative data analysis software (experience with NVivo is preferrable) Track record of emerging high-quality research outputs and potential for research leadership Strong understanding of critical approaches to urban and regional planning High level communication, collaboration and stakeholder engagement skills Demonstrated ability to work individually and in teams and build effective relationships Highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills Desired: Familiarity with NSW planning policy and the Aboriginal Land Rights Act (or ability to develop this knowledge in a timely manner) For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options Career development opportunities Cultural Leave and Indigenous Employee Network 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For more information on our staff benefits, click here. To find out more about how we support our Indigenous staff and grow their careers. Visit: Careers at UNSW for Indigenous Australians | UNSW Sydney To access our Indigenous Strategy, click here: UNSW Indigenous Strategy How to Apply: Submit your CV & a cover letter addressing your suitability for the position (no more than 3 pages) by 11:30pm on the 20th of October. We recommend using the questions below to guide your cover letter: Please describe your experience working with Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander communities in a research context. How have you ensured your approach was collaborative, ethical, and aligned with relevant frameworks such as the AIATSIS Code of Ethics and Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property principles? Provide an example of a research project you have led or contributed to that involved qualitative methods (e.g. interviews, fieldwork) and/or policy analysis. What was your role, and what were the key outcomes or impacts of the work? How does your research experience and expertise align with and/or are relevant to Indigenous planning, land use, and land development, and how would you contribute to advancing research and partnerships in this area at UNSW? Any questions? For any queries about the kind of support available for our Indigenous staff, get in touch with our Manager, Indigenous Employment, Nat Chenhall: n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the recruitment process, please contact Talent Acquisition Partner, Lucy Gerondis: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the role and your eligibility, please contact Project Director, Naama Blatman: n.blatman@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Visa sponsorship is available subject to merit-based selection. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity, preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjust Position Description Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542271/lecturersenior-lecturer-technical-cyber-security": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer -  Technical Cyber Security",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer -  Technical Cyber Security Apply now Job no: 542271 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Senior Lecturer, Lecturer Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Technical Cyber Security, UNSW Canberra Employment type: Full-time (35 hrs per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level B from $133,065 / Level C from $161,619 + leave loading + 17% super Location : UNSW Canberra About UNSW Canberra University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. Choosing a career at UNSW means embracing an environment where thriving, facing challenges, and engaging in meaningful work are not just encouraged but integral to the university experience. If you seek a career where you can excel and contribute meaningfully, you've found the right place. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Why Your Role Matters: This position in the School of Systems & Computing at UNSW Canberra is a balanced academic position that will complement the depth of expertise in cyber security in the school. You will be expected to carry out activities to develop your scholarly research and professional standing both nationally and internationally, develop and contribute significantly to educational excellence, and make a significant contribution to collegiality, engagement and leadership in the School and Faculty. A successful candidate will also contribute to the development, implementation, revision, and delivery of technical cyber security courses at undergraduate and postgraduate level as well as short courses and micro-credentials in the school. Who You Are (skills and experience): PhD in technical cyber security or a closely related discipline, with relevant professional experience. Commitment to maintaining current knowledge of developments in the discipline and profession. Demonstrated experience in teaching, curriculum design and course delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, using contemporary pedagogies, educational technologies and online methods. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, educational excellence and contribution to curriculum innovation and improvement. Commitment to supporting and inspiring students from diverse backgrounds, including through inclusive learning, meaningful assessment and effective feedback. Demonstrated research excellence through high-quality, high-impact outcomes, research leadership and, where relevant, HDR student recruitment and supervision. Experience engaging with industry or the profession, including work-integrated learning, collaboration, funding or learning and teaching initiatives. Highly developed communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, with the ability to collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Flexible working options (work from home, flexible hours etc.) can be discussed Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW Eligibility: We will consider visa sponsorship for this role. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application online before Friday 23 October 2026 at 11:30pm , including your CV and a 1–2-page cover letter addressing the required skills and experience outlined in the position description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: For recruitment process-related queries please contact: Holly Whale – Talent Acquisition Partner h.whale@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539262/talent-pool-dvc-indigenous-writer-identified": {
      "title": "Talent Pool - DVC Indigenous - Writer - Identified",
      "description": "Talent Pool - DVC Indigenous - Writer - Identified Apply now Job no: 539262 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services, Knowledge Exchange Casual Talent Pool – Writer – Identified The Pro Vice-Chancellor Indigenous Education Team of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Indigenous Division, UNSW, is seeking Expressions of Interest from Indigenous educators and/or professionals with appropriate academic qualifications for casual curriculum writer roles across the UNSW Faculties and Schools: Arts, Design & Architecture Business Engineering Law & Justice Science As part of the UNSW Progress for All Strategy, the embedding of Indigenous Knowledges in curriculum will be in focus during 2026. Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander curriculum writers will work in Faculty-based teams, supervised by an Indigenous curriculum lead in consultation with the PVC Indigenous Education to contribute to the drafting of course content representing Indigenous Knowledges within their discipline areas. They will collaborate with Faculty representatives, other subject-matter experts (SME) and key professional staff working in curriculum development to provide appropriate, high-quality representations of Indigenous knowledges in course content. This role is required to: review draft content from selected undergraduate or post graduate UNSW courses to confirm appropriate, discipline-relevant representations of Indigenous Knowledges submit drafts of proposed content for review and feedback where adjustments or new representations of Indigenous Knowledges are required in the selected courses respond productively to advice and feedback from the Indigenous curriculum lead representing a range of internal and external stakeholders provide evidence-informed, culturally responsive explanations for adjustments to draft content, assessment and support material, as required attend course-design meetings undertake any additional research (if necessary) as guided by Faculty. cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. You will need: A relevant degree and subsequent relevant experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail. Experience working on asynchronous educational content development and skills in using the Microsoft Office suite Demonstrated ability to synthesise stakeholder feedback to write and develop materials that are of high-quality, culturally responsive, clear, concise and respond to the needs of supervisors. To apply, please complete the linked application form. Pre-employment checks required for this position • Confirmation of Aboriginality. This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542972/research-associate-in-materiality-of-artificial-intelligence": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Materiality of Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Research Associate in Materiality of Artificial Intelligence Apply now Job no: 542972 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate The Opportunity The School of Minerals and Energy Resources Engineering has an opportunity for a Research Associate to investigate the mineral demand implications of scaling artificial intelligence hardware. Through scenario development, you will examine the mineral requirements of AI technologies and the geological constraints affecting mineral supply, while considering opportunities for resource efficiency and a circular economy. You will apply research methods such as material flow analysis and industrial ecology to contribute to a better understanding of the environmental and resource implications of the continued expansion of artificial intelligence. This position will provide you with the opportunity to further develop your research expertise while contributing to high-quality research outcomes. You will work independently and collaboratively, contribute to scholarly publications and research activities, engage with relevant stakeholders and research partners, participate in conferences and workshops, and assist with the supervision of research students where required. This role reports to Professor Saleem Ali and has no direct reports. Salary, Level A: AUD $118,467 to $126,711 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full time Appointment type: Fixed-term contract – 6-months Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia About UNSW UNSW is a world-leading institution recognised for its scale, prestige, and impact. With strong industry engagement and partnerships across sectors, UNSW provides a unique environment where research expertise translates into real-world outcomes. The University is home to cutting-edge research that drives innovation and addresses some of society’s most pressing challenges. For researchers, UNSW offers an outstanding platform to thrive — combining world-class facilities, collaborative networks, and a culture of innovation that supports research excellence, industry engagement, and meaningful societal impact. UNSW’s School of Minerals and Energy Resources Engineering is a global leader in mining, petroleum and sustainable energy resources engineering. Our multidisciplinary academics deliver internationally recognised research and work closely with industry to develop innovative solutions for the minerals, energy and low-carbon sectors. The School is ranked second in the world and first in Australia for Mineral & Mining Engineering, and 10th in the world and second in Australia for Petroleum Engineering in the 2025 QS World University Rankings by Subject. For more information about the School, please visit UNSW School of Minerals and Energy Resources Engineering Skills & Experience: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for this position can be found in the position description. The position description is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: For role-specific enquiries, please contact Professor Saleem Ali E: saleem.ali@unsw.edu.au For questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Eugene Aves (Talent Acquisition Partner) E: eugene.aves@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Monday, 26 October 2026 As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543483/manager-employability-external-relations-indigenous-identified": {
      "title": "Manager Employability & External Relations (Indigenous Identified)",
      "description": "Manager Employability & External Relations (Indigenous Identified) Apply now Job no: 543483 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Indigenous Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 8 $132k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (1 day WFH per week) Benefits: Cultural Leave and Indigenous Employee Network This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. Why your role matters The Employability and External Relations Manager plays an integral role within the Nura Gili Indigenous Student Success team, providing leadership to the Future Students Coordinators and driving external engagement initiatives. The role is responsible for developing and maintaining effective internal and external networks, as well as leading the Indigenous Future Students Plan to support and promote Indigenous student access, employability and success. Reporting to the Deputy Director, Nura Gili, the position has three direct reports. Specific accountabilities include: Contribute to a positive and dynamic Nura Gili community, informed by culturally affirming values, experiences and shared aspirations. Develop an Indigenous Future Students Plan and oversee its implementation, ongoing management and deliverables. Lead the Future Students Coordinators and manage day-to-day operations, including managing the activities performed by the team and managing resources deployed to meet operational and strategic objectives of the Indigenous Future Students Plan. In close collaboration with Nura Gili leadership, set and implement the strategic direction for external engagement projects, ensuring alignment with relevant initiatives and existing strategies. Oversee and evaluate external engagement activities and projects, providing additional advice and guidance to enhance outcomes as required. About You: This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. Confirmation of Aboriginality will be required. Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent lived experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Proven, extensive experience contributing to Indigenous programs and opportunities that result in positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities, including evidence of successfully improving Indigenous student outcomes. Sophisticated ability to develop and execute external relations plans to meet priorities and objectives of Indigeous student outcomes. Extensive experience in employability related to higher education or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through education/training and/or experience. Demonstrated experience in project management, planning and evaluation, with excellent ability to manage multiple tasks, establish priorities and meet deadlines. Advanced written and positive verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced and ability to liaise with all levels of internal and external stakeholders. Proven ability to lead a team towards a shared vision, strong collective outcomes and a dynamic, positive environment. Excellent organisational skills and the ability to operate autonomously while demonstrating strong initiative and a proactive approach to driving process improvement. Advanced level of computer literacy, working with a range of computer systems and applications, including Microsoft Office and customer relations databases. This role requires the successful candidate to hold a valid paid Working with Children’s Check. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Establishing your status as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person means any person who: is of Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent; identifies as an Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person; and is accepted as such by the community in which you live and or have lived. All offers of employment will be subject to prior submission of satisfactory proof of Aboriginality. For more information on what constitutes as proof of Aboriginality, please email n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: Nat Chenhall – Manager Indigenous Employment n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter before 11.55pm Sunday 18 th October 2026. In lieu of addressing the selection criteria, please answer the questions within our application form. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 30 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
