    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543738/ea-project-officer-australian-research-centre-for-cancer-survivorship": {
      "title": "EA & Project Officer- Australian Research Centre for Cancer Survivorship",
      "description": "EA & Project Officer- Australian Research Centre for Cancer Survivorship Apply now Job no: 543738 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Research Administration support, Project Management Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: 2 years fixed term Remuneration: Professional Level 6 from $108,021 per annum + 17% Super Location: Kensington, New South Wales Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary: The Executive Assistant & Project Officer provides high-level executive, project and operational support within the Australian Research Centre for Cancer Survivorship . The role delivers comprehensive executive assistance to the Centre Director, while supporting the Centre Manager with office management, administrative coordination and the delivery of key projects and initiatives. The position is responsible for managing complex enquiries, coordinating executive schedules, meetings, travel and financial administration, and building and maintaining effective relationships with senior internal and external stakeholders. The role requires a proactive and highly organised individual with strong executive support experience, excellent stakeholder management skills and the ability to exercise sound judgement, discretion and confidentiality. The successful candidate will demonstrate a high level of initiative, independence and the ability to anticipate priorities, manage competing demands and confidently manage upwards to support senior leaders effectively. Working collaboratively across the Centre, the Executive Assistant & Project Officer contributes to project coordination, process improvement and efficient office operations, supporting the Centre’s strategic priorities and operational objectives. The position reports to the Centre Manager and has no direct reports. Some Key Skills Required: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. High level organisational skills with proven ability to deal with multiple tasks, establish priorities and meet deadlines. Excellent interpersonal skills with ability to liaise effectively with all levels of staff and external stakeholders. Advanced level of computer literacy, working with a range of computer systems and applications, including Microsoft 365 suite. Superior time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Proven organisational skills and the ability to operate autonomously while demonstrating strong initiative and a proactive approach to driving process improvement. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Submit your application including your resume & selection criteria online before Sunday 8 th November at 11:55pm. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543741/digital-marketing-campaign-coordinator": {
      "title": "Digital Marketing Campaign Coordinator",
      "description": "Digital Marketing Campaign Coordinator Apply now Job no: 543741 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Marketing / Communications Employment Type: Fixed term role until January 2028 (35-hour week) Remuneration: $108K - $115K depending on experience + 17% super Location: Kensington NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) Why Your Role Matters In this role, you’ll be responsible for the successful delivery and implementation of digital marketing automation to maximise lead generation and enrolment conversion for Future Students in support of achieving international or local enrolment targets. You’ll co-ordinate prospective student communications, using marketing automation to guide prospective students from first contact to application. Working closely with recruitment, content, and marketing teams, you’ll help create landing pages, build automated workflows, and deliver seamless, data-driven campaigns. Alongside campaign execution, you’ll also be monitoring performance, optimising messaging, and reporting on outcomes to support continuous improvement. This is a fixed term role until January 2028, Monday to Friday, with hybrid working arrangements available. The position reports to the Digital Marketing Lead, and does not have any direct reports. Who you are We are looking for someone who has excellent interpersonal skills and a demonstrated ability to engage people, who can demonstrate alignment with UNSW values and comes equipped with: Experience creating email marketing and automation campaigns using platforms like ClickDimensions, Adobe Campaign, or similar Commercial acumen and understanding of digital marketing / ecommerce, with an understanding of sales funnels and activation Excellent written and verbal communication skills and the ability to liaise effectively with all levels of students, staff and external stakeholders. High level of attention to detail and ability to work under pressure and to strict deadlines Confidence in using Microsoft Office, CRM systems, and marketing automation tools Previous experience with reporting and analytics Experience working with Adobe Target, Adobe Experience Manager, Adobe Analytics and Adobe Campaign products would be desirable but not essential. Candidates will need full ongoing working rights - there is no sponsorship available for this role. We have an annual shutdown from the 22 nd of December until the 3rd of January, during which the successful person will be required to take leave; one of the benefits of working at UNSW is that all full time and part time employees get 3 extra days of leave paid over that end of the year break. How to Apply: Please submit your CV and a Cover Letter addressing why you are interested in the role and the skills and experience you bring (aligned with the \"who you are\" section of the job). You’ll also be asked an additional question in relation to your experience in the application form. Applications close: 5th November 2026 11.55PM Get in Touch: Anne de Voronine, Talent Acquisition Associate, a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 05 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543803/teaching-support-officer-medicine-teaching-support": {
      "title": "Teaching Support Officer- Medicine Teaching Support",
      "description": "Teaching Support Officer- Medicine Teaching Support Apply now Job no: 543803 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Professional Broadband Level 5/6 $92,265 - $108,021 (based on experience) + 17% super+ annual leave loading Location: Kensington Campus, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Teaching Support Officer provides high-quality administrative, logistical and operational support for undergraduate coursework teaching within UNSW’s Faculty of Medicine & Health. The role supports the effective delivery of teaching and learning activities, ensuring efficient coordination of academic processes, responsive stakeholder support and a positive experience for students and academic staff. At Level 5 , the Teaching Support Officer is responsible for coordinating day-to-day teaching support activities, including term planning, timetabling, class management, learning management systems, assessment and examination coordination, and student administration. Working collaboratively with academic and professional staff, the role ensures the smooth delivery of teaching activities, provides high-quality customer service and contributes to continuous improvement initiatives across the Faculty. At Level 6 , in addition to Level 5 responsibilities, the Teaching Support Officer provides higher-level oversight and coordination of complex teaching support processes, including academic systems, examinations, casual academic engagement and class management. The role provides specialist advice on University policies and procedures, coordinates activities across multiple stakeholders and administrative units, and contributes to process improvements that enhance operational efficiency and consistency across the Faculty. The position reports to the Team Lead and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: At Level 5: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Well-developed interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills. Advanced time management, organisational and problem-solving skills, including the ability to meet deadlines. High proficiency of computer literacy, working with a range of computer systems and applications, including Microsoft Office 365 suite including Teams, OneDrive, Excel, Word, Powerpoint and Outlook and with using UNSW’s enterprise systems. Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives. Level 6 (in addition to Level 5 above): Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and work productively as part of a team. Significant relevant experience in a similar role in a tertiary organisation. High level ability to apply knowledge and draw on significant experience to analyse and resolve complex and sensitive issues efficiently and effectively. Substantial experience in providing a broad range of teaching administration support and specialist advice to Academic staff. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Submit your application including your resume & selection criteria online before Sunday 1st November at 11:55pm. To be considered for this role, you must address the Selection Criteria which are outlined in the “Skills and Experience” section of the position description. Applications that do not address these criteria will not be considered Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543849/postdoctoral-fellow-australian-research-centre-for-cancer-survivorship-arccs": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Australian Research Centre for Cancer Survivorship (ARCCS)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Australian Research Centre for Cancer Survivorship (ARCCS) Apply now Job no: 543849 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: 1-year fixed term Remuneration: Academic Level A $118,467 per annum + 17% Super Location: Kensington, New South Wales Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Post-Doctoral Fellow (Level A) contributes to high-quality research within the Australian Research Centre for Cancer Survivorship (ARCCS) , focusing on the complexities of cancer survivorship, including work participation, costs and access to care, economic outcomes, equity in healthcare and improvements in policy and practice. The role supports the design, delivery, analysis and translation of research that aims to improve outcomes and experiences for cancer survivors. Working collaboratively with multidisciplinary researchers, cancer survivors, employers, clinicians and policymakers, the position undertakes research activities including data collection, analysis, publications and dissemination of findings. The Post-Doctoral Fellow contributes to stakeholder engagement, research co-design, grant development, research governance and project coordination, while supporting student supervision and the Centre’s broader research priorities. The successful candidate will demonstrate strong research and analytical capabilities, initiative and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively. The role offers opportunities to contribute to impactful research, build interdisciplinary partnerships and translate research findings into meaningful improvements in cancer survivorship care, policy and practice. The position reports to the Centre Director and has no direct reports. Some Key Skills Required: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. This could include public health, health services research, epidemiology, behavioural science, psychology, rehabilitation science, implementation science, occupational health, or a related field. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Submit your application including your resume & selection criteria online before Sunday 8 th November at 11:55pm. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543859/postdoctoral-fellow": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow Apply now Job no: 543859 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Chemistry teaches more than 2000 undergraduate students each year from many different faculties and offers four different undergraduate programs leading to majors in chemistry that are taught in five teaching laboratories specialising in First Year, Synthetic, Analytical and Physical Chemistry. This role and contributes to the development of novel electrocatalysts and ion exchange membranes for electrolyzers and fuel cells. The Postdoctoral Fellow is also responsible for synthesising, prototyping, and scaled-up demonstration, along with assessing key metrics of efficiency and durability, relating these metrics to product performance and life cycle cost, and developing relevant experiments under schedule constraints. THIS ROLE IS LCOATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. About the role $118K - $126K plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2 years Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute independently or as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes in the discipline area. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create scholarly output that is recognised by peers, including by preparing manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets. Undertake other discipline-appropriate research activities, e.g. surveys, literature reviews, data gathering and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Prepare and maintain experimental materials, equipment, and data. Prototype electrochemical devices based on research results. Actively apply for external grants and fellowships with support from the supervisor. Assist with lab management, including instrument maintenance, troubleshooting, and contacting service providers when required. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in Chemistry, Chemical Engineering or Materials Science. Demonstrated research experience or expertise in the following: Ion exchange membranes for electrochemical energy conversion; Material synthesis and device assembly; and Material characterisation. Prior experience in DFT calculations is preferred but not compulsory. Demonstrated experience in conducting a research project (including experiment design, data collection, and record-keeping) and coordinating the efforts of a large multidisciplinary team. Demonstrated ability to complete projects in a timely manner and submit reports. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications in high-impact journals and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Demonstrated experience in student supervision. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed above in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Chuan Zhao E: chuan.zhao@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 29 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543906/research-associate-school-of-photovoltaic-and-renewable-energy-engineering": {
      "title": "Research Associate- School of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering",
      "description": "Research Associate- School of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering Apply now Job no: 543906 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate Employment Type : Full Time, 35 hours per week Duration : Fixed term until 31 Dec 2027 Remuneration : Research Academic Level A6-A8 from $118,467 per annum + 17% Super + Annual Leave Loading Location : Kensington, Sydney, New South Wales Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position . The School of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering is internationally recognised for its record-breaking research in solar power (photovoltaics) and renewable energy. The PERC solar cell was first invented at UNSW in our labs in 1983 and today powers more than 85% of all new solar panel modules all over the world. SPREE’s work and people have changed the face of sustainable energy on the global stage, and we continue to be at the forefront of leading-edge research and development in the field of renewable technology as our economies transition away from fossil fuels. For more information, please see the following link: School of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering | Engineering - UNSW Sydney Position Summary: The Research Associate (Level A) contributes to UNSW’s research excellence through the development of innovative molecular devices based on singlet fission, an emerging photophysical process with the potential to enhance solar-energy conversion and advance optoelectronic technologies. The role focuses on developing advanced spin-physics models and applying magnetic-field-dependent spectroscopy techniques to investigate, quantify and control the spin dynamics underpinning singlet fission, contributing directly to A/Prof. Murad Tayebjee’s ARC Future Fellowship research. Working collaboratively with researchers and academic colleagues, the Research Associate undertakes theoretical and experimental research, analyses and interprets complex data, and contributes to the development of design principles for efficient singlet fission materials and devices. The position also supports the dissemination of research findings through scholarly publications, conferences and workshops, while providing technical and theoretical guidance to PhD students and contributing to the broader research activities of the group. The Research Associate reports to A/Prof. Murad Tayebjee and has no direct reports. Some Key Skills Required: Experience modelling the spin physics of triplet excitons Experience working with magnetic field dependent imaging and modulated magnetic field spectroscopy techniques Experience working in a multidisciplinary team of physicists, chemists, and engineers A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: 17% Superannuation and leave loading Flexible working An additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period Access to lifelong learning and career development Progressive HR practices More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Aarti: Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Sunday 1 st November  before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543979/senior-project-officer": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer",
      "description": "Senior Project Officer Apply now Job no: 543979 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration The National Centre of Excellence in Intellectual Disability Health aims to improve health outcomes for people with intellectual disability. We are seeking a Senior Project Officer with excellent research, synthesis and writing skills. You will bring together findings from the Centres reports to inform advocacy to strengthen research capacity for inclusive intellectual disability health research. You will use the Centre’s Strengthening Research Capacity Strategy as the framework for synthesising findings from recent reports including: Audit of Intellectual Disability Health Research Funding in Australia What is Important to Us in Health Research? Establishing National Health Research Priorities Through a Co-Design Process First Nations and Intellectual Disability Inclusive Research Report You will develop clear, evidence-informed messages to support advocacy for: greater recognition of intellectual disability health as a research priority increased investment from the Medical Research Future Fund (MRFF) and the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC) funding processes that support inclusive research meaningful involvement of people with intellectual disability in research. This role will also: • Monitor the delivery of projects and initiatives. • Contribute to the development of project plans, schedules. • Identify and provide solutions to project risks and issues. • Liaise with project stakeholders to ensure project metrics including time, cost and quality are being met within defined deliverables and deadlines. • Provide strategic and detailed project support and advice to project team and stakeholders. • Ensure projects are managed consistently and adheres to UNSW governance requirements. • Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. A person engaged is generally expected to have the following skills and experience: • A relevant tertiary qualification and extensive project management experience. • Proven experience in the development of project plans, objectives, documentation and demonstrated understanding of the project life cycle. • Ability to influence stakeholders. • Advanced level of computer literacy and skills in Microsoft Office applications and project related databases and management systems. To submit an application for the role, please do so via the 'Apply Now' button. Please note, no applications will be accepted via email. Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. If you require any further information regarding the role, please contact Jane Lloyd j.lloyd@unsw.edu.au #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/540369/program-manager-ai": {
      "title": "Program Manager (AI)",
      "description": "Program Manager (AI) Apply now Job no: 540369 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: 18 months fixed term role as a Program Manager ( AI ), UNSW IT Attractive salary package available Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community—but more importantly, it’s a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. It’s this approach that makes our work matter—and why we’re recognised as one of the world’s top 20 universities and ranked Australia’s #1 university , as well as a proud member of the prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re looking for a role where you can shape change, thrive in complexity, and see the real impact of your work every day , you’re in the right place. The Program Manager (AI) is a senior leadership role responsible for the strategic planning, governance, integration and successful delivery of UNSW’s AI initiatives across the University. The role leads and directs the planning, development and delivery of UNSW’s AI initiatives within the Innovation & Enablement team. The role operates alongside the Technology Manager (AI), providing strategic leadership and program direction, supporting the Head of AI Ecosystem in establishing and executing a university-wide AI delivery roadmap. It ensures that all activities are aligned with the UNSW AI Services & Governance Framework, University technology standards, and relevant privacy, security and ethical obligations.  Working collaboratively with delivery streams across business analysis, integration, solution architecture and engineering, the Program Manager (AI) provides structure, reporting and assurance for Responsible AI delivery across AWS and Azure environments.  This position reports to the Head of AI Ecosystem and has direct reports. You will lead and provide strategic oversight for the planning, execution and governance of AI initiatives across the university in collaboration with the Technology Manager (AI) and delivery leads.  Collaborate with the Head of AI Ecosystem in developing a AI program master plan that oversees program schedules, key milestones, interdependencies, budgets, benefits realisation and change management activities.  Lead and oversee program-level risk reviews, privacy impact assessments and assurance activities to ensure responsible and compliant AI implementation across the University.  Establish and maintain program-level governance artefacts, registers and dashboards, ensuring accuracy and transparency.  Lead stakeholder engagement and communications across faculties, divisions and executive leadership, preparing high-quality program updates, strategy papers and steering committee reports. Oversee delivery dependencies, milestones and outcomes across multiple AI projects and operational initiatives, ensuring alignment with the University’s strategic objectives. Manage program resources, budgets and delivery performance, ensuring financial responsibility, quality outcomes and realisation of program benefits. Work closely with the Technology Manager (AI) to align technical delivery progress with governance requirements.  Lead, mentor and develop program staff and workstream leads, fostering a high-performing, collaborative and inclusive program environment. Who you are: Relevant tertiary qualifications and/or equivalent experience in program or project management in large complex technology environments. Demonstrated experience leading complex, large-scale programs that include AI, data or digital transformation components. Understanding of the Responsible AI lifecycle, from feasibility to deployment, including risk management, evaluation and compliance. Familiarity with AWS and Azure cloud ecosystems and integration with enterprise systems. Extensive experience leading and influencing multi-disciplinary teams and delivery partners (architecture, data, engineering, cyber, business). Extensive experience establishing and managing program governance, reporting frameworks, risk management practices and quality assurance processes. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, including the ability to brief senior stakeholders and synthesise complex information clearly. Experience in Agile or hybrid delivery environments using Jira and Confluence. Knowledge of the UNSW AI Services & Governance Framework and demonstrated commitment to Responsible AI principles. Strong analytical and organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise concurrent initiatives. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture - Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc), Career development opportunities, Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period, Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Wednesday 4th of November at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543066/lead-developer": {
      "title": "Lead Developer",
      "description": "Lead Developer Apply now Job no: 543066 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst Employment Type: 4 roles available -  24 months, fixed term role as a Lead Developer ] Starting salary: $153,933 plus 17% super and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community—but more importantly, it’s a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. It’s this approach that makes our work matter—and why we’re recognised as one of the world’s top 20 universities and ranked Australia’s #1 university , as well as a proud member of the prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re looking for a role where you can shape change, thrive in complexity, and see the real impact of your work every day , you’re in the right place. About the role: Reporting to the Technology Manager, you will lead the design, development and support of high-quality technology solutions that meet business needs and deliver a strong user experience. You will provide technical leadership across the full solution lifecycle, from design and development through to testing, deployment and ongoing support, ensuring solutions are secure, scalable and aligned with organisational standards. Working closely with business and technology stakeholders, you will translate functional requirements into effective technical solutions, providing guidance on system design and development best practices. You will play a key role in operations and project delivery, managing technical risks and issues, supporting governance and change processes, and ensuring solutions are delivered on time and to a high standard. This role offers the opportunity to influence technology outcomes across the organisation, driving continuous improvement, fostering collaboration, and contributing to the successful delivery and ongoing optimisation of critical business systems. Who you are: Strong experience in PeopleTools and related technologies (SQL, App Designer, App Engine, CI, IB, Workflow, Fluid etc) Solid understanding of PeopleSoft HCM (Core HR, Position Management, Administer Training, Absence Management, Time & Labour, and Global Payroll for Australia) Understanding and hands on experience with Oracle Fusion and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure would be desirable Strong experience with working on complex L3 enhancements and fixes (10-15yrs+ experience) Extensive experience using delivery management tools (e.g. JIRA) Comprehensive analytical and problem-solving skills, proven capacity to exercise initiative, flexible and proactive in development of robust solutions Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to Benefits and Culture - Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) , Career development opportunities, 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments , Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period, Di scounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Wednesday 4th of November at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543175/senior-business-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Business Analyst Apply now Job no: 543175 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst Employment Type: 24 months, fixed term role, Senior Business Analyst, UNSW IT Starting salary: $153,933 plus 17% super and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW and the role: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community—but more importantly, it’s a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. It’s this approach that makes our work matter—and why we’re recognized as one of the world’s top 20 universities and ranked Australia’s #1 university, as well as a proud member of the prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re looking for a role where you can shape change, thrive in complexity, and see the real impact of your work every day, you’re in the right place. This role plays a lead role within the Human Resources subdomain of the UNSW IT Corporate Services directorate. The role is responsible for leading and conducting business analysis activities on assigned projects and business-as-usual change requests The incumbent will be required to partner with the business and other IT teams within the university to provide specialist advice and guidance to ensure the sound and effective execution of projects and operational work. The role reports to the Lead Business Analyst – Human Resources and has no direct reports. Lead and deliver business analysis activities across HR technology projects and operational initiatives, partnering with stakeholders to identify , analyse and document business requirements, processes and solution needs. Facilitate workshops, interviews and discovery activities, applying appropriate analysis and modelling techniques to define clear, fit-for-purpose business solutions. Provide day-to-day leadership and guidance to Business Analysts, supporting effective delivery, capability development and continuous improvement of business analysis practices. Build strong stakeholder relationships, manage risks and issues, and contribute to successful project outcomes by ensuring requirements are well understood, effectively communicated and aligned to business objectives . Who are you: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Extensive experience in business analysis tools such as Enterprise Architect and yEd. Extensive experience in business analysis notations such as E/R diagramming, UML and BPMN. Demonstrated ability to build executable prototypes. Sound consultation, influencing and negotiation skills and proven ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders to achieve successful outcomes. Comprehensive analytical and problem-solving skills and proven capacity to exercise initiative, flexibility and to be proactive in development of robust solutions to problems. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Sound experience in leading and mentoring a team ensuring growth in both breadth and depth of role, tasks assigned and role modelling the UNSW behaviours. Demonstrated success working effectively and collaboratively on initiatives with a range of people at different levels within an organisation. Knowledge of budgeting and resource allocation procedures. High level of experience working with Microsoft Office modules, including Word, Excel and Powerpoint. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture - Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc), Career development opportunities, 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments , A dditional 3 days of leave over Christmas period, Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Wednesday 4th of November at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543412/project-officer": {
      "title": "Project Officer",
      "description": "Project Officer Apply now Job no: 543412 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management Employment Type: Part Time, 28 Hours Per Week Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 6/7 - $108,021 - $128, 233 + 17% super Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid working) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Division of Societal Impact, Equity and Engagement (DSIEE) leads UNSW's efforts to create positive and lasting impact through research, education and community engagement. Embedded within the Faculty of Arts, Design & Architecture (ADA), the External Engagement team works to amplify the faculty's research, education and student experience through strategic communications, events, stakeholder engagement and digital initiatives. Reporting to the Senior External Engagement Manager, the Project Officer supports the planning, coordination and delivery of projects, communications, events and engagement initiatives across the Faculty. Working with a diverse range of stakeholders, the role contributes to the successful delivery of activities that support faculty priorities and external engagement objectives. Your focus: Deliver strategic projects, communications and events that engage key audiences and demonstrate the societal impact of UNSW Arts, Design & Architecture. As a Project Officer you will: Coordinate and deliver a diverse portfolio of projects, communications and engagement initiatives that support Faculty priorities. Support the planning and delivery of events, activations and stakeholder engagement activities across the Faculty. Develop and deliver communications materials, campaigns and content for a range of internal and external audiences. Manage project timelines, milestones, deliverables and reporting requirements to ensure successful outcomes. Coordinate website updates and digital content, ensuring information remains accurate, engaging and aligned with University standards. Build productive relationships with stakeholders across the Faculty, University and external community. Monitor project performance and contribute to evaluation, reporting and continuous improvement activities. Support compliance with UNSW governance, brand and approval processes across all projects and initiatives. Who you are: You are a highly organised and proactive professional who enjoys working across a diverse portfolio of projects, communications and events. You are equally comfortable collaborating as part of a team and working autonomously, and you bring strong communication skills, sound judgement and a commitment to delivering high-quality outcomes. This role would suit someone with demonstrated experience supporting or delivering events, projects and stakeholder engagement initiatives within a complex organisation. You enjoy building relationships, managing competing priorities and taking ownership of projects from planning through to delivery and evaluation. You will bring the following skills and experience: Relevant tertiary qualifications, or an equivalent combination of education, training and professional experience. Demonstrated experience managing or coordinating projects, including planning, implementation, monitoring and evaluation. Experience supporting or delivering events, engagement activities and communications initiatives from planning through to execution. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to develop audience-focused communications and build effective stakeholder relationships. Strong project management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. Experience working with a broad range of stakeholders and navigating complex organisational environments. Experience using event and communications platforms such as Eventbrite and Mailchimp. Proficiency across a range of digital platforms and applications, including Adobe Creative Suite and website content management systems. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively within a team while exercising initiative and working independently. Experience within higher education, government, creative industries, galleries, fundraising, events or stakeholder engagement environments will be highly regarded. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the position description (found on job listing on JOBS@UNSW). How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter (required) detailing your interest and suitability for the job before Sunday 8th November,  by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: For queries on the recruitment process, please contact Rachel Darcy: r.darcy@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contacts above cannot be accepted. #LI-KM1 Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543860/senior-admin-and-project-officer-school-of-materials-science-engineering": {
      "title": "Senior Admin and Project officer – School of Materials Science & Engineering",
      "description": "Senior Admin and Project officer – School of Materials Science & Engineering Apply now Job no: 543860 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Project Management $118483-$128,233 depending on experience (level 7) + 17% Superannuation Full Time Permanent UNSW Kensington Campus (hybrid) The School of Materials Science and Engineering is one of the top ranked Materials programs in the Asia-Pacific regions and strongly positioned in every facet of materials science and engineering. It offers undergraduate programs in Materials Science and Engineering (Honours), as well as a number of combined degree programs with Chemical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering and Commerce. About the role A Senior Admin and Project Officer plays a key role within the School in providing high level practical and efficient project support for strategic and operational School Projects. The role entails generally administrative support and project management, reporting and analytical support, developing communications, building key internal and external relationships, and developing/ consulting on key programs and projects. The role work closely with the School Manager and Head of School to support operational activities and identify areas for improvements and process transformation. The position reports to the School Manager and has no direct reports. Accountabilities include: Develop and monitor the delivery of School projects and initiatives to achieve operational and strategic goals and overall administrative duties Oversee the development of project plans and schedules and participate in planning processes as required Support operational activities and identify areas for improvement and process transformation Provide a high level of proactive project support to project team and stakeholders such as School Executive Team Develop and manage digital infrastructure and communications to support process improvements and strategic initiatives within the School Monitor, track and report on the status of project deliverables to ensure time, cost and quality metrics are in line with approved project plans for assigned projects Assess and manage project risks and issues and provide solutions where applicable Develop and manage effective communications with key stakeholders, both internal and external to the project About you To be successful in this role, you will be a strong team player and come with: Demonstrated administrative and project experience, with an understanding of the project life cycle and development of project plans, objectives and documentation Previous experience in a higher education sector is highly desirable Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced Demonstrated success building effective working relationships through delivering outcomes and collaboration with diverse stakeholders across an organisation Excellent stakeholder management skills, with the ability to liaise effectively with a range of stakeholders, including high profile industry members Experience working with a range of computer systems and applications, including MS Office Suite of applications including Excel, Copilot, Sharepoint, PowerBI, Power Automate, Adobe Enterprise Management Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands This is a permanent, full-time hybrid role, with a flexible start date in either December 2026 or late January 2027. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Application documents: please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your motivation for applying and addressing the “About You” section of this job ad. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Contact: Anne de Voronine, Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 8th November 11.55PM ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au Position Description Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543923/academic-engagement-librarian": {
      "title": "Academic Engagement Librarian",
      "description": "Academic Engagement Librarian Apply now Job no: 543923 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Library Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: 12 Month Fixed Term Contract Remuneration: Level 6 $108k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW Why your role matters The Academic Engagement Librarian plays a key role in supporting UNSW’s education, research and engagement objectives by connecting academics, researchers and Higher Degree Research (HDR) candidates with Library services and resources. Working collaboratively with Faculties, Schools and key stakeholders across the University, the role delivers high-quality teaching, research support and outreach services that enhance digital literacy, academic success and research capabilities. Drawing on strong disciplinary knowledge and an understanding of the higher education sector, the position contributes to the development and delivery of tailored workshops, consultations and curriculum-integrated learning initiatives, ensuring the UNSW community has access to high-quality Library support and expertise. The role reports to the Team Leader, Academic Engagement, and has no direct reports. About You: Our ideal candidate should possess a strong service orientation, excellent collaboration and communication skills. You should be able to work independently and in teams and enjoy providing guidance and support to a diverse range of stakeholders, including Library academic engagement staff, faculty members, researchers and students as well as: A relevant degree from a higher education or research institution, with subsequent relevant experience or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Eligibility for associate membership of the Australian Library and Information Association would be highly regarded. Demonstrated experience in delivering in-person, online and hybrid consultations and workshops in higher education, research or similar environments. Excellent interpersonal, negotiation and client liaison skills with demonstrated ability to influence and sustain relationships. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team and across units, and to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Demonstrated experience using innovative technologies to enhance library services for digital information literacy and online learning. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV and a Cover Letter addressing your suitability to the role. You will also be asked to answer the below questions within the application from. Role closes at 11.55pm Thursday 5 th November 2026. How will your skills, experience and approach help you succeed as an Academic Engagement Librarian? Learning and teaching practices continue to evolve, particularly with changes in educational technology and generative AI. How would you identify emerging student needs and adapt Library services and resources to support their academic journey in consultation with the faculty? Effective learning and teaching support often depends on strong relationships with academic staff and an understanding of their curriculum priorities. How would you build relationships with course convenors and teaching academics and Identify opportunities to embed Library support into courses. Job queries contact: Belinda Sua b.sua@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 05 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543949/senior-category-manager-technology": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager - Technology",
      "description": "Senior Category Manager - Technology Apply now Job no: 543949 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Procurement Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term contract until 29 November 2028 Remuneration: (Level 9) $153,933 – $163,338 + 17% superannuation + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Senior Category Manager, Technology plays a key role in delivering strategic procurement outcomes across UNSW, developing and executing sourcing strategies aligned with the University's strategic priorities. Working across the IT portfolio, you will lead complex strategic sourcing initiatives, manage commercial negotiations and provide expert procurement advice to senior stakeholders. You will work collaboratively with faculties and divisions to understand business needs, optimise commercial value and proactively manage procurement and supply risks. Reporting to the Procurement Manager, this position has no direct reports but provides leadership, coaching and mentoring to category teams across portfolios, supporting the successful delivery of complex procurement initiatives. Responsibilities Summary: Lead strategic technology sourcing projects aligned with the UNSW Strategy, coordinating cross-functional teams and engaging executive-level stakeholders. Develop and implement category plans, sourcing strategies and procurement roadmaps in collaboration with the Procurement Manager. Lead market engagement and sourcing events to deliver competitive procurement outcomes. Negotiate and manage complex commercial contracts, ensuring value optimisation, risk mitigation and compliance. Provide expert commercial advice on sourcing strategies, negotiation approaches, supplier performance and procurement risks. Build and maintain effective relationships with senior internal and external stakeholders, suppliers and industry bodies. Mentor and coach category team members, supporting problem solving and stakeholder relationship management. Maintain accurate procurement data, contract records and reporting to track benefits, pipeline activity and contractual obligations. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualification with extensive relevant experience, or equivalent competency gained through education, training or experience. Chartered Institute of Purchasing & Supply (MCIPS) accreditation is desirable. Experience managing major IT procurement projects, including coordinating procurement activities across multiple technology categories. Demonstrated end-to-end technology procurement experience across a broad range of categories including systems integration, telecommunications, Saas, networking, and IT infrastructure, with experience managing procurement involving multinational technology vendors. Extensive procurement experience, including the application of leading-edge technologies, innovative solutions and emerging technology trends. Demonstrated experience developing and implementing procurement strategies that deliver cost savings, process improvements and enhanced performance. Proven expertise in negotiating and managing complex commercial agreements, with a strong focus on risk mitigation and value optimisation. Outstanding interpersonal, negotiation, influencing and relationship-building skills, with the ability to engage senior stakeholders. Demonstrated strategic planning capability, including developing and implementing effective operational plans. Demonstrated ability to adopt innovative procurement methods, challenge existing processes and deliver commercially astute outcomes. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, customer service orientation and problem-solving capability. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a cover letter outlining your suitability for the position in relation to the selection criteria (listed under the ‘skills & experience’ section above) via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 8 th of November. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks : As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543019/lead-business-analyst": {
      "title": "Lead Business Analyst",
      "description": "Lead Business Analyst Apply now Job no: 543019 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst Employment Type: Full time, fixed term role for 2 years as a Lead Business Analyst – HR Solutions, UNSW IT E xcellent salary package available Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. About the Role The Lead Business Analyst – HR Solutions is a key leadership role within an award-winning team recognised for its high engagement, strong performance and commitment to innovation. Reporting to the Head of HR Solutions and leading a team of four to five Business and Functional Analysts, you will play a critical role in shaping and delivering HR technology solutions that support the University's strategic objectives. You will lead the analysis, design and continuous improvement of complex HR technology solutions, providing domain leadership and technical guidance across a diverse portfolio of projects and operational initiatives. Working at the intersection of business and technology, you will translate strategic and operational requirements into practical, scalable solutions that improve processes, systems and employee experiences. You will play a leading role in the ongoing evolution and optimisation of the University's enterprise HR technology landscape, including Oracle Fusion HCM and associated HR platforms. As a trusted leader and advisor, you will build strong relationships across HR, Finance, IT, vendors and governance forums, ensuring solutions are aligned to business priorities while effectively managing operational, security, privacy, compliance and change risks. You will also drive continuous improvement of standards, practices and service outcomes, while building team capability and fostering a culture of collaboration, accountability and excellence. This is an outstanding opportunity for an experienced leader who enjoys solving complex business challenges through technology and influencing HR technology strategy within a large and dynamic organisation. Who you are: You are an experienced people leader who enjoys developing high-performing teams and partnering with stakeholders to solve complex business challenges. You bring deep experience with enterprise HR technology platforms and their broader integration, reporting and data landscapes. You are confident influencing senior stakeholders, constructively challenging assumptions, and driving positive outcomes in complex organisational environments. You combine strong analytical thinking with a pragmatic approach to problem solving, ensuring solutions address both immediate needs and long-term objectives . You understand how technology solutions are delivered, with experience across different delivery methodologies and software development lifecycles. You have successfully established or matured business analysis and functional design capabilities, driving consistency, quality and continuous improvement. You bring recent hands-on and application leadership experience with PeopleSoft HCM and Payroll in a complex enterprise environment. Experience with Oracle Fusion and ServiceNow, including implementation, optimisation or support of business processes and technology solutions, would be highly regarded. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc.) Career development opportunities Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Monday 2 nd of November at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543045/senior-functional-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Functional Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Functional Analyst Apply now Job no: 543045 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: 3 roles available, all 24 months fixed term, Senior Functional Analyst, UNSW IT Starting salary $153,933 plus 17% super and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community—but more importantly, it’s a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. It’s this approach that makes our work matter—and why we’re recognised as one of the world’s top 20 universities and ranked Australia’s #1 university , as well as a proud member of the prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re looking for a role where you can shape change, thrive in complexity, and see the real impact of your work every day , you’re in the right place. About the role: The Senior Functional Analyst will provide functional design and configuration, drawing upon their expertise and experience, to deliver the design and development of solutions that are aligned to meet current and future needs, ensure they are integrated across the organisation and established methods and standards are followed. This position reports to the Lead Business Analyst and has no direct reports. Lead the design, configuration and continuous improvement of HR systems, partnering with stakeholders to deliver solutions that meet business needs and maximise the value of PeopleSoft, Oracle Fusion and related technologies. Drive process improvements, provide expert functional advice and support system enhancements that improve the effectiveness, efficiency and user experience of HR services. Provide advanced functional support for HR systems, ensuring their performance, integrity and ongoing optimisation . Collaborate with business users, project teams and technical specialists to assess requirements, implement system changes, manage testing and releases, and support the successful delivery of projects and operational initiatives. Build and maintain strong stakeholder relationships, contributing to effective change management, user adoption and business outcomes through clear communication, impact assessment, guidance and continuous improvement of system capabilities and processes. Who you are: Tertiary qualifications in a relevant discipline, or equivalent experience including a minimum of 10 years of experience in IT. At least 5 years of functional experience with PeopleSoft HCM applications in business analyst, functional consultant or business systems support role, in a large and complex organisation, ideally in higher education. Solid understanding of PeopleSoft HCM (Core HR, Position Management, Time & Labour, Absence Management and Global Payroll for Australia) Sound understanding and hands on experience with Oracle Fusion Strong experience with working on complex L3 enhancements and fixes (10-15yrs+ experience) Extensive experience using a delivery management tool (e.g. JIRA) High-level knowledge and competency in the use of professional business analysis techniques and methods. Well-developed skills in the use of standard office toolsets including word processors, spreadsheets, drawing/diagramming tools (e.g. Visio), presentation tools (e.g. PowerPoint) and project management tools (e.g. MS Project). Sound consultation, influencing and negotiation skills and proven ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders to achieve successful outcomes. Comprehensive analytical and problem-solving skills and proven capacity to exercise initiative, flexibility and to be proactive in development of robust solutions to problems Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Monday 2 nd of November at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543071/deputy-director-defence-research-institute": {
      "title": "Deputy Director, Defence Research Institute",
      "description": "Deputy Director, Defence Research Institute Apply now Job no: 543071 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW, Canberra, ACT Categories: Professional Leadership, Business Development / Industry Engagement, Knowledge Exchange, Management Employment Type : Full time Duration: Continuing position Remuneration: Senior Appointment Location: UNSW Canberra or Sydney campus Why your role matters The Deputy Director, Defence Research Institute (DRI), supports the Director in providing strategic leadership and direction for UNSW’s defence and national security research activities, fostering partnerships and translating the University’s research expertise, innovation and education capabilities into tangible outcomes for Defence, defence industry and the broader national security community. The role focuses on building strong partnerships across academia, industry and government, identifying and developing research collaboration opportunities, and overseeing the Institute’s operations, programs and projects. The position works closely with UNSW researchers and external partners to strengthen UNSW’s contribution to defence innovation and capability. The role reports to the Director, DRI and has two direct reports. About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Flexible working options (work from home, flexible hours etc.) can be discussed Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW About you: You have a relevant postgraduate qualification and or equivalent experience, along with extensive management experience in Defence or a defence- related sector. You bring strong stakeholder management, communication and negotiation skills, with a demonstrated ability to build influential networks and lead initiatives across diverse stakeholder groups. You also have proven capability in strategic advice, project delivery, risk and budget management, with a track record of successfully delivering multiple complex projects. You will demonstrate a commitment to UNSW’s values and health and safety responsibilities. Security clearance: The successful candidate must hold a minimum Baseline Australian Government security clearance. Candidates holding a current NV1 clearance are highly desirable. How to apply: Please submit your CV and Cover Letter via the Apply button before 11.55pm, Sunday 1 st November 2026 . As part of the application process, you will also be asked to respond to two short free-format questions designed to help us better understand your experience.  A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact : Shiree Thomas – Talent Acquisition Partner: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contact person will not be accepted NSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-ST1 Position Description Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543088/research-assistant-centre-for-molecular-oncology": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Centre for Molecular Oncology)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Centre for Molecular Oncology) Apply now Job no: 543088 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Administration support, Clinical/Medical Research Research Assistant Multiple positions (Centre for Molecular Oncology) Employment Type: Full - time (35 hours a week) Positions: Multiple positions available across different teams, see details below. Duration: Fixed Term until 31 st December 2027 (possible extension, funding dependant) Remuneration : $92,265 base, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: Kensington, Sydney (hybrid working model, 3 days on-site) The Organisation The Centre for Molecular Oncology is a new research centre established at UNSW which is dedicated to advancing the understanding and treatment of cancer. The Centre applies basic, translational and clinical approaches to understanding cancer biology through patient-centred discovery programs and uses this information to improve patient outcomes through clinical trials. The Research Assistant plays a key role, specifically to assist in the coordination of activities related to the Cancer Screening Program (CaSP) and the SMOC surveillance study nationally. This may include contact with patients, liaison with clinicians and medical services, data quality checking and study reporting. The position involves maintaining a high level of accuracy, attention to detail and professional communication when handling patient information and coordinating research activities. The types of roles we have available and specific skills required We are looking for multiple Research Assistants to work within individual teams (see summary below). In the application process, you will be asked to select your preferred team(s) based on your skillset and interest. Full position descriptions for each role are attached to this advert, please fully review the requirements before making a selection. CaSP Molecular Oncology Board (MOB) team: you will assist with the coordination of activities related to the Omico MOB, including the review of clinico-pathological patient data and dissemination of comprehensive genomic profiling (CGP) results and MOB reports to referring medical oncologists. Candidates with excellent written communication skills and a science-based undergraduate degree, particularly with an understanding of biochemistry and/or molecular biology, will be highly regarded. CaSP Referrals team: you will assist with the co-ordination of activities related to clinician referrals to CaSP, including the review of clinico-pathological patient data and communicating with referring clinicians.  Candidates with excellent communication skills, prior clinical experience and a science-based undergraduate degree, particularly with an understanding of molecular biology, will be highly regarded. CaSP Consents team: you will assist with the co-ordination of activities related to consenting patients to CaSP for comprehensive genomic profiling, including communicating with patients and family members.  Candidates with excellent communication skills, prior experience with patients and a relevant undergraduate degree will be highly regarded. Research requests team: you will assist with the co-ordination of activities related to researchers seeking access to biobanked biospecimens and associated data. Candidates with excellent written communication skills and a science-based undergraduate degree, particularly with an understanding of biochemistry and/or molecular biology, will be highly regarded. Surveillance study (SMOC) team: you will assist with the co-ordination of activities related to the SMOC whole body MRI surveillance study including co-ordinating patient appointments, clinical data collection and data entry and biospecimen collection. Candidates with excellent communication skills, prior experience with patients and a relevant undergraduate degree will be highly regarded. Some key skills required: Relevant tertiary qualification or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training or experience. Demonstrated experience maintaining complex databases and performing high-volume data entry with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Demonstrated experience in the implementation, coordination and management of clinical research projects will be highly regarded. Review and interpret clinical, pathological and patient information to support research activities, ensuring data is accurate. Accurately enter, maintain and validate patient and research data, ensuring the integrity, quality and security of research records and databases. Excellent written communication skills, with a high level of interpersonal skills to engage effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders. Demonstrated and applied knowledge/understanding of HREC and governance applications. Demonstrated skills in project management, software packages (e.g., Office Suite). Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contacts for each role: Beverley Murrow (MOB team) - b.murrow@unsw.edu.au Isabelle Danos (Consents team) - i.danos@unsw.edu.au Laura Manuel (Referrals and Research Requests) - laura.manuel@unsw.edu.au Ellie Carpenter (SMOC team) – ellie.carpenter@unsw.edu.au Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Wednesday 4 th November before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543388/lecturer-medicine": {
      "title": "Lecturer- Medicine",
      "description": "Lecturer- Medicine Apply now Job no: 543388 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer Employment Type: Part Time, 0.2 FTE (7 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Academic Level B from $133,065 pro rata per annum + 17% Super + Clinical Loading Location: The Sutherland Hospital, New South Wales Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Lecturer in Medicine is an Education Focussed role responsible for delivering high-quality teaching, clinical education and student support within UNSW’s undergraduate Medicine program. Primarily based at Sutherland Hospital, the role contributes to the coordination and practical delivery of internal medicine training in accordance with the guidelines of the Medicine Program and Discipline of Medicine at the St George and Sutherland Clinical Campuses. The position supports educational excellence through innovative teaching practices, the development of learning activities and assessments, student engagement and academic quality assurance. Working collaboratively with clinical and academic colleagues, the Lecturer contributes to enhancing student learning experiences, supporting academic progression and preparing future medical professionals for clinical practice. The Lecturer reports to the Head of Clinical Campus, St George and Sutherland Clinical Campuses and to the relevant Head of Discipline. The employed academic will receive direction from the Director of Medical Education and has no subordinate direct reports. Some Key Skills Required: Hold current registration with AHPRA as a medical practitioner. Have achieved Fellowship of a relevant Australasian Specialist Medical College. A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Maintain a clinical appointment at South-Eastern Sydney Local Health District (SESLHD) to facilitate teaching in the clinical setting. Actively stay up to date with discipline knowledge and professional development. Demonstrated experience in teaching and learning design, development and delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level. Experience using and/or designing teaching with educational technologies and online delivery methods. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Submit your application including your resume & selection criteria online before Wednesday 4 th November at 11:55pm. To be considered for this role, you must address the Selection Criteria which are outlined in the “Skills and Experience” section of the position description. Applications that do not address these criteria will not be considered Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543603/data-engineer": {
      "title": "Data Engineer",
      "description": "Data Engineer Apply now Job no: 543603 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: Full time, 35 hours per week, continuing position. Remuneration: Level 7– from $118,483 + 17% superannuation. Location: Kensington, NSW. Hybrid work profile. About the Role The Enterprise Data Office (EDO) enables data-informed decision-making across UNSW by managing its enterprise data platform, analytics, reporting, and governance capabilities to deliver secure, high-quality data for strategic, operational, and regulatory needs. Reporting to the Head of Data Product & Insights with no direct reports, the Data Engineer helps design, deliver, and enhance EDO’s Enterprise Data Intelligence Platform (EDIP). This encompasses data ingestion, transformation, modelling, integration, and operational support. Operating with substantial technical expertise and independence, the role resolves complex data engineering challenges, adapts established methods, and creates tailored solutions. Additionally, the position contributes to technical standards, advises stakeholders, and offers technical guidance, peer reviews, and work coordination for team members and delivery partners. The successful candidate will have demonstrated experience in and be responsible for: Independently design, build, test, and maintain secure, scalable data pipelines and models aligned with business requirements. Apply advanced data engineering techniques to build curated, reusable data structures supporting enterprise reporting and analytics. Resolve complex data quality, integration, and performance issues using fit-for-purpose methods and robust solution architecture. Lead technical work packages from scope and testing through to deployment and production issue resolution within set timeframes. Provide expert consultative advice to EDO colleagues and UNSW stakeholders on data modelling, platform capabilities, and technical constraints. Partner with stakeholders and delivery partners to capture requirements, balance competing interests, and improve technical standards through peer reviews and knowledge sharing. About You Tertiary qualifications in IT or equivalent experience in complex enterprise data engineering, with desirable exposure to Microsoft Azure and Databricks. Sound knowledge of data governance, security, and Higher Education reporting frameworks including TCSI, TEQSA, and QILT. Practical expertise building data engineering solutions using SQL, Python/PySpark, Azure Data Factory, Databricks, and API/ETL/ELT patterns. Proven experience in data modelling and lakehouse architecture, incorporating Kimball methodology, dimensional modelling, and performance optimisation. Strong analytical skills to resolve complex technical issues, adapt established approaches, and deliver practical, fit-for-purpose solutions. Excellent communication, stakeholder management, and DevOps capabilities (CI/CD, Azure DevOps, Git, Unity Catalog) tailored for Agile environments. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before Wednesday 4 November by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543891/senior-manager-equity-engagement-partnerships": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Equity, Engagement & Partnerships",
      "description": "Senior Manager, Equity, Engagement & Partnerships Apply now Job no: 543891 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Entrepreneurship, Management One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The Senior Manager, Equity, Engagement & Partnerships provides senior professional leadership across the Faculty of Science’s Equity, Engagement and Partnerships (EE&P) portfolio. Working in partnership with the portfolio’s academic and strategic leadership, including the Deputy Dean, Equity, Engagement & Partnerships, and the Associate Deans, the Senior Manager translates agreed strategic priorities into an integrated program of work and leads the planning, development and delivery of complex, cross-functional projects, programs and initiatives within the Faculty. The Senior Manager drives the integration and delivery of work across the EE&P portfolio, bringing together work led by specialist managers and project leads. The role builds effective relationships across the Faculty and University, influences stakeholders to achieve shared outcomes, and works closely with the External Engagements Business Partner and their team to align Faculty and divisional engagement priorities. It identifies and resolves barriers to delivery and provides high-level advice and recommendations to the Portfolio Leadership Team. The role ensures that priorities are connected, delivered effectively and evaluated for impact. About the role $153k - $163k plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Continuing Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Drive the integration and delivery of the EE&P portfolio, bringing together workstreams, priorities and dependencies across specialist managers, project leads and relevant Faculty and University teams. Lead the planning, development and delivery of complex, cross-functional projects and initiatives that translate agreed EE&P priorities into operational plans and outcomes. Provide high-level professional advice and recommendations to the Portfolio Leadership Team and Faculty leadership on opportunities, risks, emerging issues and matters requiring action or decision. Plan and take a leading role in liaising, consulting and negotiating the development, modification and implementation of programs and practices at Faculty level, as relevant to the EE&P portfolio. Prepare high-quality presentations, briefing materials and decision papers to support the Portfolio Leadership Team in progressing EE&P priorities. Build and maintain effective relationships across the Faculty, University and relevant external networks, including working closely with the Senior External Engagement Manager and their team to align Faculty and divisional engagement priorities. Establish clear planning, decision-making and delivery arrangements for EE&P priorities, ensuring actions are progressed, issues are escalated and outcomes are monitored and evaluated. Lead the design and delivery of initiatives that strengthen engagement with staff, students and external partners in support of the Faculty’s EE&P priorities. Lead and manage direct reports within the EE&P portfolio, supporting clear priorities, high performance, professional development and continuous improvement. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Ensure hazards and risks, psychosocial and physical, are identified and controlled for tasks, projects and activities that pose a health and safety risk within the area of responsibility. About the successful applicant (Selection criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A degree in a relevant field combined with extensive management expertise and supporting experience within a complex organisation or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Substantial experience and subject matter expertise in one or more relevant fields, such as engagement, communications, partnerships, or equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience across several of these areas, preferably in higher education or another complex organisation, would be desirable. Significant experience in strategic planning and leading the delivery of complex, cross-functional projects, programs or portfolios, with a demonstrated ability to translate priorities into practical outcomes. Demonstrated ability to provide high-level advice and recommendations to senior leaders, exercise sound judgement and support effective decision-making. Proven experience leading and developing staff, including direct reports, and fostering a collaborative, high-performing team culture. Highly developed stakeholder engagement, consultation, influencing and negotiation skills, with a proven ability to build relationships with diverse stakeholders and successfully lead through influence across organisational boundaries. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills and a proven capacity to identify opportunities, be proactive in development of robust solutions to problems, manage risks and dependencies, and respond effectively to emerging issues. High-level organisational and time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to competing and changing priorities, manage multiple tasks, work independently and deliver high-quality outcomes within competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Demonstrated success working collaboratively across a complex, matrixed organisation and building effective relationships with a range of internal and external partners and stakeholders at different levels within an organisation. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced and a demonstrated ability to prepare clear and persuasive advice for senior audiences. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed . Contact: Veneet Sood E: v.sood@unsw.edu.au Applications close: November 18 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542689/associate-business-systems-analyst": {
      "title": "Associate Business Systems Analyst",
      "description": "Associate Business Systems Analyst Apply now Job no: 542689 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Full-time continuing role as an Associate Business Systems Analyst, UNSW IT Starting Salary $118,483 plus generous superannuation and leave loading Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Associate Business Systems Analyst applies business and systems analysis expertise to support the delivery and improvement of business systems across the University, particularly those related to student services and administration. The role plays a key part in contributing to business and functional analysis, system testing, and data preparation activities to support system operations, system enhancements and project delivery. Working with a high degree of independence, under broad direction of senior team members, the position facilitates across business analysis, system analysis, and integration activities. It focuses on building foundational capability in systems knowledge, analysis techniques, and delivery practices while contributing to high-quality outcomes. Accountabilities: Play a key role in business and functional analysis activities, including gathering, documenting, and validating requirements under guidance. Analyse and evaluate business processes, workflows, and system behaviours identifying opportunities for improvement and optimisation. Prepare and execute system and integration test scripts, including capturing results and supporting defect resolution ensuring quality outcomes across complex scenarios. Gather, analyse and interpret data for reporting, and decision-making purposes and conduct the data preparation tasks for testing. Work collaboratively with senior analysts, developers, integration and other relevant teams to support delivery activities and provide practical support where required. Develop and maintain documentation such as process flows, test cases, system artefacts, and other delivery materials. Diagnose and resolve complex functional issues, applying analytical judgement and recommending solutions. Maintain and apply in-depth knowledge of student systems and their interactions with other university systems and platforms. Maintain a structured and inquisitive approach to analysis and testing activities, validating assumptions, identifying constraints and edge cases, and seeking guidance where information is incomplete or unclear. Identify and drive continuous improvement initiatives, exploring emerging industry, technology trends and using contemporary tools and digital ways of working including AI where appropriate, under guidance from senior team members. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. Who you are: Tertiary qualifications in a relevant discipline or equivalent combination of education and professional experience. Relevant experience in a business analysis, systems support, testing, or related role, or strong graduate capability with relevant exposure. Strong understanding of business analysis concepts, system functionality, and delivery practices. Demonstrated experience in applying standard approaches to documenting business and system processes, such as BPMN, Flowcharting, UML models, etc. Experience in system testing, including the development and execution of test cases, recording outcomes, and supporting defect tracking. Strong attention to detail and ability to follow structured processes and documentation standards. Good communication and collaboration skills, with the ability to engage stakeholders and provide advice across business and technical teams. Strong demonstrated ability to think critically, adopting a methodical and structured approaches to problem-solving, with a willingness to question assumptions, identify gaps, and learn through investigation in complex environments. Demonstrated willingness and capability to learn and apply modern digital tools, contemporary delivery practices, and emerging technologies including AI in a practical and responsible way. Familiarity with standard office and collaboration toolsets such as MS Office, Excel, or diagramming tools. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: please apply through the portal, we would like you to submit a full application including resume and addressing the who you are section. Applications close:  Sunday 1st of November at 11.30pm Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Jen MacLachlan j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Partner – UNSW IT Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542690/business-systems-analyst": {
      "title": "Business Systems Analyst",
      "description": "Business Systems Analyst Apply now Job no: 542690 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst Full-time continuing role as a Business Systems Analyst, UNSW IT. Starting Salary $132,445 plus generous superannuation and leave loading Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Business Systems Analyst collaborates with stakeholders across the University to analyse business and functional needs, define system requirements, and support the delivery, operations and improvement of business systems, particularly within the student services domain. The role operates with a solid degree of autonomy and accountability, leading analysis activities, contributing to solution design, and supporting system testing and integration efforts. The position develops strong functional knowledge of the systems it supports and applies that knowledge to deliver effective and sustainable business outcomes. Accountabilities: Lead stakeholder engagement to understand business challenges, gather requirements, and translate them into clear and structured outputs. Analyse and document business processes, identifying opportunities for optimisation and improved system utilisation across the university. Lead functional analysis for assigned systems, developing strong knowledge of system capabilities and dependencies. Drive and influence solution design by working closely with technical teams to ensure alignment with business needs. Plan and strategize system and integration testing activities, including test design, execution oversight, and defect management. Perform data analysis to support reporting, validation, and business decision-making, informing strategic and operational improvements. Apply critical thinking and sound judgement in analysis and testing by validating assumptions, identifying constraints, dependencies, risks, and edge cases, and proactively seeking clarification or additional information where required. Maintain key documentation including requirements, process flows, system artefacts, and testing outputs. Advise and guide system enhancements, vendor interactions, implementation activities, and practical collaboration with developers where required. Oversee functional support and complex issue resolution, ensuring effective triage, escalation, and root cause analysis. Work closely with stakeholders to build strong relationships, agree on outcomes, and balance business and technical priorities. Mentor and support associate analysts, providing expertise on analysis and delivery activities, working to contribute to capability uplift within the team. Contribute to continuous improvement by exploring emerging industry, technology trends. Apply contemporary business analysis practices, digital tools, and emerging technologies including AI where appropriate to improve analysis quality, delivery efficiency, and business outcomes. Ensure compliance with university policies, data governance, security standards, and modern ways of working that support innovation and continuous improvement and contribute to the development and refinement of these standards where required. • Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or other. Who you are: Tertiary qualifications in a relevant discipline or equivalent combination of education and professional experience. Extensive experience in business analysis, systems analysis, or a related role in a complex environment. Demonstrated experience in requirements gathering, process analysis, functional analysis, and solution support including ownership of outcomes and delivery of system improvements. Experience supporting system and integration testing activities, including test design, execution, and defect management. • Highly developed stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate, and provide specialist advice across business and technical domains. Demonstrated ability to work with a solid degree of autonomy and accountability, with high attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities, all whilst contributing to team delivery outcomes. Exposure to standard approaches to documenting business and system processes, such as BPMN, Flowcharting, UML models, etc. • Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to question assumptions, work through ambiguity, identify constraints and edge cases, and ensure solutions are fit for purpose in complex business and system contexts. Experience with enterprise systems or platforms such as student systems, CRM, workflow tools, or related platforms is desirable. • Exposure to data analysis or reporting tools such as SQL, Power BI, or equivalent is desirable. Demonstrated ability to use contemporary digital tools, business analysis practices, and emerging technologies including AI where appropriate to support efficient, evidence-based, and high-quality delivery outcomes. Familiarity with Agile, Waterfall, or hybrid delivery methodologies. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: please apply through the portal, we would like you to submit a full application including resume and addressing the who you are section. Applications close:  Sunday 1st of November at 6.30pm Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW Get in Touch: Jen MacLachlan j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Partner – UNSW IT Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543303/administrator-unsw-defence-industry-security-program-disp": {
      "title": "Administrator, UNSW Defence Industry Security Program (DISP)",
      "description": "Administrator, UNSW Defence Industry Security Program (DISP) Apply now Job no: 543303 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Administration, Governance, Policy & Compliance Administrator, UNSW Defence Industry Security Program (DISP), UNSW Canberra Employment type: Full-time, 35 hrs pw Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Broadband 5/6, Level 5 from $92,265, Level 6 from $108,021 + leave loading + 17% super Location : UNSW Canberra, ADFA Campus Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must be Australian Citizens to be considered for this position. About UNSW Canberra University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as Australia’s #1 university, one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. Choosing a career at UNSW means embracing an environment where thriving, facing challenges, and engaging in meaningful work are not just encouraged but integral to the university experience. If you seek a career where you can excel and contribute meaningfully, you've found the right place. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW Canberra operates under a long-standing core agreement with the Department of Defence to provide academic and higher education programs at the Australian Defence Force Academy (ADFA), a partnership that has spanned over 50 years. As part of this agreement, UNSW Canberra maintains institutional engagement with the Defence Industry Security Program to support its campus-wide security protocols, research partnerships, and governance workflows. In this role you will provide vital support for our DISP team. Who You Are (skills and experience): At Level 5 • Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. • Advanced time management, organisational and problem-solving skills, including the ability to meet deadlines. • Well-developed interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills. • Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. • Highly proficient computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications. • Ability to make sound judgements and work both independently and as part of a team. • Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives. At Level 6 (in addition to the above) • Excellent time management and organisational skills, and the ability to work well under pressure and manage multiple tasks with competing deadlines. • Demonstrated ability to guide staff. • Highly advanced interpersonal, written and oral communication skills. • Ability to plan, organise and co-ordinate projects and tasks. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Flexible working options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW Eligibility: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must be Australian Citizens to be considered for this position in order to achieve an Australian Government security clearance at the Baseline level, along with the ability to obtain and maintain a Negative Vetting Level 1. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application online before Monday 2 November 2026 at 11:30pm , including your CV and a 1–2-page cover letter addressing the required skills and experience outlined in the position description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: For role-related queries please contact: Rachel Martens - Defence Security Manager r.martens@unsw.edu.au For recruitment process-related queries please contact: Holly Whale – Talent Acquisition Partner h.whale@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. #LI-HW1 Position Description Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543584/research-fellow": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow Apply now Job no: 543584 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Research Associate Research Fellow Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level B $133,065 - $156,864 (depending on experience) + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About us: The UNSW Centre for Social Impact (CSI) is an education and research centre based at UNSW Business School. Our mission is to develop people and partnerships that accelerate social innovations that build the impact economy. Through engaged scholarship, we work collaboratively with communities, organisations and governments to address complex social and environmental challenges and create practical solutions that contribute to social impact. We are part of UNSW Business School, a top business school in the Asia–Pacific region, with a global reputation for innovation, research excellence and teaching that embeds real business and community challenges into curricula. At our core are exceptional academics and researchers, alongside students dedicated to advancing social, economic and environmental progress for future generations. Position Summary: A Research Fellow (Level B) is a valued member of the research team, and expected to carry out both independent and collaborative research that supports the CSI research strategy pillars of investment, innovation, and strategy and evaluation of social impact. They will produce both academic and practitioner outputs from research projects, diffusing knowledge to ensure it contributes to economic and social prosperity. The Research Fellow reports to the Research & Innovation Director and has no direct reports. A current focus of the role will be participating in and supporting Chief Investigators of an Australian Research Council Discovery Project investigating the s trengthening of regional economies through place-based investment. Key Responsibilities: Conduct research/scholarly activities under limited supervision, either independently or as a member of a team. Create scholarly impact in the discipline which is recognised by peers in the advancement of disciplinary knowledge, including high-quality academic publications and public contributions Contribute to the strategy of the Centre of Social Impact, through the design and development of applications for competitive funding for research Participate as co-investigator or chief investigator in competitive grant applications, or show evidence of active participation in research collaborations funded by competitive grants. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets. Undertake research activities and field work, e.g. surveys, literature reviews, data gathering (including interviewing) and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Participate in and/or present at conferences and/or workshops relevant to projects as required. Assist with the supervision of research students and assistants in the research area where required. Provide leadership and support for project management and administration to achieve deliverables, including organising and attending place-based focus groups and meetings with participants Skills and Experience: A PhD in a business-related discipline, including management, organisation studies, strategy, entrepreneurship, and sociology, with expertise in qualitative and mixed methodologies Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments, especially relevant to impact investing, regional economic development, and/or civic wealth creation. Demonstrated track record in research with outcomes of high quality and high impact with clear evidence of the desire and ability to continually achieve research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. A track record of significant involvement with the profession and/or industry in conducting research projects with academic and industry relevant outcomes. High level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students, staff, industry, and community partners Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships, especially with external partners. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter by 11.55pm Tuesday 3 rd November 2026 Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Contact: Amy Denney a.denney@unsw.edu.au - Talent Acquisition Partner Please apply directly through the website , applications sent to the contact above will not be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543629/research-assistant-identified": {
      "title": "Research Assistant- Identified",
      "description": "Research Assistant- Identified Apply now Job no: 543629 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Clinical/Medical Research, Indigenous Research Assistant (The Kirby Institute) - Identified Employment Type: Part - time (17.5 hours a week) Duration: Fixed term until 31 st May 2030 Remuneration : $92,265 base pro rata, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading UNSW offers up to 10 days paid culture leave annually for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff members Location: Remote available and/or Sydney. This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of an Indigenous workforce. About The Kirby Institute: The Kirby Institute is a world-leading health research institute at UNSW Sydney. We work to eliminate infectious diseases, globally. Our specialisation is in developing health solutions for the most at-risk communities. Putting communities at the heart of our research, we develop tests, treatments, cures and prevention strategies that have the greatest chance of success. Details of the role The Research Assistant (Identified) is an identified role, as Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander. The position is based within the Yandamanjang First Nations Health Research Program and will work closely with the Biosecurity Program to support EPIWATCH®, contributing to culturally informed epidemic intelligence research and strengthening infectious disease surveillance relevant to First Nations communities. The role will receive leadership and support through the Yandamanjang Program while undertaking day-to-day research activities as part of the multidisciplinary EPIWATCH® team for example by translating Aboriginal languages. The role reports to the head of the Yandamanjang First Nations Health Research Program and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and lived experience, in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health research, public health or a related discipline. Knowledge and experience in the “Top End” languages (Warlpiri, Yolngu Matha [Yolŋu languages] and Kriol) and cultures. Assist with translation of key search terms into priority Aboriginal languages (Warlpiri, Yolngu Matha [Yolŋu languages] and Kriol) in collaboration with the EPIWATCH® AI team. Knowledge of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultural protocols when engaging with communities. Demonstrated experience working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and stakeholders, with the ability to build respectful and culturally safe relationships. Liaise with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander stakeholders across Australia and coordinate consultations to identify infectious disease outbreaks of importance to First Nations communities, including those involving antimicrobial resistance. Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to research, community engagement or project activities, preferably in a health or research setting. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including:​ 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Up to 10 days paid culture leave annually for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff members Indigenous Employee Network Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. This role requires Confirmation/Evidencing Aboriginality. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Application instructions: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a cover letter briefly addressing the skills and experience listed in the position description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact For more information on how UNSW contributes to Indigenous initiatives, please contact Nat Chenhall, Manager of Indigenous Employment on Email: n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au For more information on UNSW’s recruitment process please contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate on Email: Frank.McHenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Monday 2 nd November 2026 before 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543817/general-administrator-school-of-computer-science-and-engineering": {
      "title": "General Administrator – School of Computer Science and Engineering",
      "description": "General Administrator – School of Computer Science and Engineering Apply now Job no: 543817 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration The School of Computer Science and Engineering at UNSW is seeking a General Administrator to provide coverage while a current team member is seconded to another position. You will provide day-to-day administrative and facilities support, including responding to stakeholder enquiries, maintaining records and databases, coordinating meetings and events, and assisting with purchasing, travel and reimbursements. You will also support facilities and space-related matters, recruitment and onboarding activities, and process improvement initiatives. You will bring relevant administrative experience, strong organisational and communication skills, and the ability to manage competing priorities. A customer-focused approach, sound judgement, confidence using Microsoft Office and the ability to work independently and as part of a team will be important to your success. This role reports to the Deputy School Manager, with a dotted reporting line to the Team Lead – General Administrator. Salary: Level 5 – $92,265 to $104,401 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full-time Appointment type: Fixed-term appointment to provide secondment coverage, with an ASAP start until December 2027 Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia Work arrangement: This is a campus-based role requiring five days per week onsite, with the potential to move to four days per week onsite. About Us UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged, and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The School of Computer Science and Engineering is one of the largest and most prestigious schools of computing in Australia. It offers undergraduate programmes in Software Engineering, Computer Engineering, Computer Science, and Bioinformatics, as well as a number of combined degrees with other disciplines. Our research and teaching staff are world leading and world building as they advance knowledge and learning. For more information about our School go to the following link: https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/our-schools/computer-science-and-engineering Skills and Experience Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Advanced time management, organisational and problem-solving skills, including the ability to meet deadlines. Demonstrated administrative, facilities, procurement and/or store activities experience within a large and complex institution or equivalent. Demonstrated ability to apply, interpret, advise on and contribute to the development of protocols, procedures, guidelines and administrative systems. Well-developed interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Highly proficient computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications. Ability to make sound judgements and work both independently and as part of a team. Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives. Advanced numeracy skills. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Additional details about the specific responsibilities of this position can be found in the position description, which is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply: Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV, cover letter and responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. To be considered, applicants must have full working rights in Australia and be able to work on-site at our Kensington campus. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: For role-specific inquiries, please contact Sherry Gao (Deputy School Manager) E: sherry.gao@unsw.edu.au For questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Eugene Aves (Talent Acquisition Partner) E: eugene.aves@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Sunday, 1 November 2026 As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543903/manager-leadership-support-faculty-of-science": {
