    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543678/centre-administrator-arc-centre-of-excellence-for-quantum-computer-performance-and-integration": {
      "title": "Centre Administrator - ARC Centre of Excellence for Quantum Computer Performance and Integration",
      "description": "Centre Administrator - ARC Centre of Excellence for Quantum Computer Performance and Integration Apply now Job no: 543678 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration The Opportunity The Centre Administrator provides day-to-day administrative support to ensure all services are provided efficiently to meet the operating needs of the ARC Centre of Excellence in Quantum Computer Performance & Integration (QPI). The role has particular responsibility for the day-to-day maintenance of QPI's central operational database, built on the Zoho Creator platform, which the Centre relies on to record, track and report on its activities across eight partner-university nodes. As a member of the QPI Operations Team, the Centre Administrator will work closely with node administrators and professional staff across the Centre's eight partner-university nodes, helping to maintain consistent record-keeping and administrative practice across QPI's national network. The Centre Administrator will report to the Chief Operations Officer and has no direct reports. We would welcome applications from candidates seeking either full-time or part-time work (0.6 FTE or above). Finding the right person matters more to us than a particular working pattern, and we will work with the successful candidate on an arrangement that suits them. Salary, Level 5: $92,265 - $104,401 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full time or Part time (0.6 to 1.0 FTE considered) Appointment type: Fixed-term contract – 3 years Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia (on campus requirement) About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. About QPI The ARC Centre of Excellence for Quantum Computer Performance and Integration (QPI) is a multi-institutional research initiative bringing together researchers, students and industry partners to advance useful quantum computing while building the capability and culture that will shape Australia's quantum future. QPI is hosted at UNSW Sydney within the Faculty of Engineering, and its research program spans eight Australian university nodes: UNSW, the University of Sydney, the University of Technology Sydney, the Australian National University, Griffith University, the University of Queensland, RMIT University and the University of Melbourne together with government agencies, research organisations and industry partners across the quantum computing sector. The Centre is led by a Director and three Deputy Directors, each responsible for one of QPI's priority areas: Research Excellence, People & Culture and Innovation & Translation. For more information on the Centre, visit: https://www.qpi.org.au Alongside its research objectives, QPI is committed to shaping the culture of Australia's emerging quantum technology workforce: training a new generation of quantum research leaders, embedding equity and inclusion at every level of the Centre and fostering a culture of scientific integrity and collaborative practice across the program. Skills and Experience Completion of a relevant certificate or diploma-level qualification, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training Demonstrated experience maintaining data within databases or similar business systems, with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Well-developed interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills including a demonstrated ability to liaise effectively with diverse groups within and external to the organisation, and to work cooperatively with colleagues across the organisation Excellent organisational and time management skills, with the ability to set priorities and meet multiple deadlines while remaining flexible and responsive to organisational needs Demonstrated administrative skills, including experience in the provision of executive assistance to senior staff, sound financial administration practices, and demonstrated skills in governance support and record-keeping Sound computer skills, including databases, word processing, spreadsheets and email An understanding of and commitment to UNSW's aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Desirable Experience working in a university, research institute or multi-institutional research centre environment Experience using Zoho Creator or a similar low-code database or CRM platform Experience supporting research student, HR or research administration processes Demonstrated experience in event planning, management and delivery Additional details about the specific responsibilities for this position can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW. We recognise that there is no single pathway to success in this role. We welcome applicants with experience gained in universities, research organisations, industry, government or the not-for-profit sector. We value backgrounds spanning researcher development, education, people and culture, program delivery, stakeholder engagement and community building. If you meet most of the criteria and believe your experience could contribute to QPI’s success, we encourage you to apply. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offers a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including:​ 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working/Hybrid working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. To Apply: Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV and One-page Cover Letter Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. To be considered, applicants must have full working rights in Australia and be able to work on-site at our Kensington campus. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: For role-specific inquiries, please contact Monica Brockmyre (Chief Operating Officer) E: m.brockmyre@unsw.edu.au For questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Allyssar Hamoud (Talent Acquisition Associate) E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on 2 nd November 2026 As part of our recruitment process, candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/540564/technology-specialist-lead-ai": {
      "title": "Technology Specialist Lead (AI)",
      "description": "Technology Specialist Lead (AI) Apply now Job no: 540564 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst Employment Type: 24 months, fixed term role as a Technology Specialist ( AI ), UNSW IT Starting salary: $153,933 plus 17% super and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community—but more importantly, it’s a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. It’s this approach that makes our work matter—and why we’re recognised as one of the world’s top 20 universities and ranked Australia’s #1 university , as well as a proud member of the prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re looking for a role where you can shape change, thrive in complexity, and see the real impact of your work every day , you’re in the right place. The Technology Specialist Lead (AI) leads the designs, development, and maintenance of AI-enabled applications and integration components that support UNSW’s digital transformation and major technology initiatives. Working within the Innovation & Enablement team, this role applies advanced specialist engineering expertise and provides technical leadership build and operate retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), LLM-based APIs, and AI data pipelines on AWS and Azure. The position ensures all engineering work aligns to the UNSW AI Services & Governance Framework, UNSW Cyber and privacy standards, and agile delivery principles, whilst contributing to the development and improvement of AI engineering standards and practices. This position reports to the Technology Manager (AI) and has no direct reports. You will lead the design, develop and maintenance of AI-enabled solutions, including RAG pipelines, vector database integrations, and LLM-based APIs on AWS and Azure across multiple projects or programs University-wide. Collaborate with Developers and peers to ensure secure, efficient and responsible AI engineering practices in line with UNSW standards, providing specialist guidance and technical leadership. Lead and implement assigned AI engineering tasks within agile sprints, contributing to backlog refinement and technical reviews, including providing expert input into solution design and prioritisation. Prepare unit and functional tests for AI components, including automated prompt evaluations and safety tests (toxicity, hallucination, latency), ensuring compliance with quality and risk requirements. Execute testing and evaluation of AI pipelines against defined KPIs (accuracy, relevance, safety) and support continuous improvement across the university. Lead, deliver and maintain AI and integration solutions, ensuring observability, traceability, and compliance with Responsible AI controls and organisational governance requirements. Develop runbooks, documentation and end-user guidance for AI-integrated features; promote awareness of safe and ethical AI use across stakeholder groups. Who you are: Relevant tertiary qualifications in Computer Science, Engineering, or IT, or equivalent experience. Demonstrated experience developing solutions using AWS and Azure services, including AI/ML APIs, data pipelines, and microservices in complex enterprise environments. Experience integrating with LLM APIs, RAG pipelines, vector databases, and related frameworks. Strong programming ability in Python, TypeScript or C#, with experience in CI/CD pipelines and containerisation (Docker, GitHub Actions, or Azure DevOps). Understanding of GenAIOps and LLMOps workflows, including prompt management, model evaluation, and telemetry at scale. Proven ability to apply privacy and security best practices, including data minimisation, redaction, and encryption in alignment with organisational governance requirements. Demonstrated agile delivery experience (Scrum/Kanban) and proficiency in using Jira and Confluence. Excellent analytical and problem-solving ability with strong debugging and optimisation skills to resolve complex technical issues. Strong interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills; able to explain AI systems to non technical stakeholders and provide high-level technical advice. Knowledge of the UNSW AI Services & Governance Framework and commitment to Responsible AI practices. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Sunday 1st of November 2026 at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543024/postdoctoral-fellow-arc-discovery-project": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Fellow - ARC Discovery Project",
      "description": "Post-doctoral Fellow - ARC Discovery Project Apply now Job no: 543024 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate, Senior Research Associate Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term contract from May 2027 until 3 November 2028 Remuneration: Level B: $133,065 - $156,864 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Paddington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation but we are a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: A Post-Doctoral Fellow is sought to join the ARC Discovery Project Artists and Generative AI: Copyright and Private Regulation of Creativity . You will undertake systematic computational analysis of generative AI system behaviours, examining a range of generative AI systems and datasets, including systems for tracing content or “inspiration” attribution. As a Post-Doctoral Fellow (Level B), you will carry out independent and collaborative research and apply your research expertise in Machine Learning, Data Science or other forms of high-performance computational analysis. The role reports to Prof Oliver Bown, School of Art & Design, and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Undertake computational experiments in AI media generation using UNSW and national compute infrastructure, and perform quantitative and qualitative analysis in line with the project research design. Liaise with UNSW ResTech support personnel and relevant professional staff to manage access to compute infrastructure. Gather and organise relevant data with appropriate copyright, privacy and security controls, in accordance with UNSW data management policies and human ethics principles. Contribute to the design of research to fulfil the aims of the ARC Discovery Project. Conduct independent and collaborative research under limited supervision, including leading authorship on high-quality research publications. Create scholarly impact through research outputs that advance disciplinary knowledge. Contribute to HDR supervision and the mentoring and guidance of students and colleagues, where requested. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: A PhD in a related discipline, including AI, Computer Science, Data Science, a scientific discipline involving quantitative data analysis, or a Creative Arts or Social Science discipline where a high degree of computing and data processing proficiency can be demonstrated. Familiarity with Machine Learning principles and current Generative AI practices. Demonstrated experience relevant to the project, such as creating and training ML systems; benchmarking or analysing ML systems; systematic computational analysis of cultural materials such as art, music or literature; building datasets; managing high-performance computing applications; or building AI agents. Ability to program in a relevant programming language for data and media analysis. Demonstrated research track record with high-quality, high-impact outcomes, evidence of the ability to achieve research excellence and capacity for research leadership. High-level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students and staff. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a 1-2 page cover letter, and a separate document addressing your suitability for the role in relation to each the selection criteria (listed under ‘skills & experience’ on the PD) via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 7 th of December. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . UNSW is unable to sponsor international candidates for this position. Applicants must have unlimited Australian work rights for the duration of the contract. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the role, please contact Professor Oliver Bown: o.bown@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks : As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. (#LI-DNI) Position Description Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 07 Dec 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543281/development-officer-future-giving": {
      "title": "Development Officer, Future Giving",
      "description": "Development Officer, Future Giving Apply now Job no: 543281 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Fundraising / Philanthropy Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 7: $118,483 - $128,233 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Division of Societal Impact, Equity and Engagement connects UNSW with core stakeholders including philanthropists, alumni, government, volunteers, the media and the broader community. As the Development Officer, Future Giving, you will proactively support the Development team to help build UNSW’s fundraising program. You will implement strategies to achieve significant philanthropic revenue targets and manage a portfolio of approximately 100 planned and major gift prospects, with a focus on cultivating and soliciting major and planned gifts in support of the University’s highest fundraising priorities. The role reports to the Senior Development Manager, Future Giving and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Support the philanthropic strategy for the Faculty/Division to achieve agreed strategies and targets. Work collaboratively with the Development team and support the future giving program by providing advice, information and support to the Faculty/Division. Build and manage a portfolio of approximately 100 planned and major gift prospects, developing strong and sustainable relationships to identify and deepen their interest in UNSW. Prepare proposals, solicitation letters and cultivation and stewardship materials for planned gift prospects and donors. Collaborate with development colleagues and academic leaders to develop gift ideas and strategies and foster productive relationships for UNSW. Liaise with the Donor Services & Estates Officer on realised bequests and work with external stakeholders, UNSW Legal Counsel and Finance. Develop and implement an annual work plan to maximise effectiveness and meet activity targets, including prospect visits and dollar goals. Maintain excellent data records and tracking reports for planned gift prospects and donors, and provide high-quality advice and support to colleagues. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Support the philanthropic strategy for the Faculty/Division to achieve agreed strategies and targets. Work collaboratively with the Development team and support the future giving program by providing advice, information and support to the Faculty/Division. Build and manage a portfolio of approximately 100 planned and major gift prospects, developing strong and sustainable relationships to identify and deepen their interest in UNSW. Prepare proposals, solicitation letters and cultivation and stewardship materials for planned gift prospects and donors. Collaborate with development colleagues and academic leaders to develop gift ideas and strategies and foster productive relationships for UNSW. Liaise with the Donor Services & Estates Officer on realised bequests and work with external stakeholders, UNSW Legal Counsel and Finance. Develop and implement an annual work plan to maximise effectiveness and meet activity targets, including prospect visits and dollar goals. Maintain excellent data records and tracking reports for planned gift prospects and donors, and provide high-quality advice and support to colleagues. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a 1-2 page cover letter addressing your suitability for the role in relation to each the selection criteria (listed under ‘skills & experience’ on the PD) via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 5 th of November. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks : As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 05 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543474/senior-integration-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Integration Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Integration Analyst Apply now Job no: 543474 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst Employment Type: Full Time, 2 Year Fixed-Term opportunity. Remuneration: Level 9: From $153,933 + 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington with transition to newly renovated office at our Randwick campus. About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role The Senior Integration Analyst is responsible for maintaining the core integration platform and developing integration services. This includes implementing new integration requirements and providing support across organisational projects. The position reports directly to the Lead Integration Solutions Architect and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Lead the hands-on development, configuration, and delivery of core integration components using the Microsoft technology stack and Azure platform, either directly or via internal IT teams and vendors. Design, build, and document robust web service interfaces and integration logic (including orchestration and transformation) that align logical requirements with target performance and security standards. Collaborate with architects and application owners to review software for compliance with UNSW integration standards and architecture governance. Guide developers, internal organisational units, and suppliers throughout the implementation lifecycle, ensuring smooth infrastructure and integration platform changes. Establish clear specifications, acceptance criteria, and comprehensive test scenarios to validate that updated processes deliver operational efficiencies and business benefits. Proactively manage complex risks, issues, and resourcing while supporting leadership to optimise team dynamics and application integration workflows. Drive continuous improvement initiatives and manage key internal and external stakeholder communications to achieve strategic outcomes. About You Tertiary qualifications in IT or a related discipline, combined with 5+ years of software development experience leveraging cloud architectures and middleware components. Proven expertise in applying synchronous and asynchronous integration patterns (API, ESB, ETL) alongside managing design practices, registers, and system landscape diagrams. Extensive hands-on experience designing and deploying solutions on Microsoft Azure, covering Integration Services (API Management, Event Grid, Logic Apps, Service Bus, Function Apps) and Data Platform components (Data Factory, Data Lake, Databricks). Practical experience in planning, designing, and deploying Azure IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS environments, including network, security frameworks, DevOps, and CI/CD pipelines. Strong technical background in API development (REST/JSON, SOAP/XML, Web Services) with a deep understanding of application, API security, and privacy controls. Comprehensive knowledge of full-lifecycle system integration methods, QA/testing practices, and delivery within hybrid Agile and Waterfall environments. Outstanding analytical, problem-solving, and communication skills, with a track record of collaborating across all organisational levels to manage competing priorities effectively. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before 11:55pm Sunday 1 November 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543487/program-manager-leap": {
      "title": "Program Manager, Leap",
      "description": "Program Manager, Leap Apply now Job no: 543487 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Project Management Employment Type: 14 months fixed term role as a Program Manager, UNSW IT. Key leadership role within transformation of our technology strategy Attractive salary package available Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community—but more importantly, it’s a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. It’s this approach that makes our work matter—and why we’re recognised as one of the world’s top 20 universities and ranked Australia’s #1 university, as well as a proud member of the prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re looking for a role where you can shape change, thrive in complexity, and see the real impact of your work every day, you’re in the right place. The Program Manager will play a key leadership role in the planning and execution of the UNSW Technology Strategy, Leap As part of a small Leap delivery team, the role will apply strong program management discipline to maintain momentum and progress agreed priorities with stakeholders, across IT, distributed technology owners and the wider University. This requires broad knowledge of technology, including digital delivery, AI and emerging technologies, and a strong customer focus, with an understanding of how business needs and technology delivery connect. The Program Manager will ensure University stakeholders responsible for progressing the strategy have the structures, reporting, tools and ongoing support and leadership to progress the strategy on a day-to-day basis. The Program Manager will have direct reports, and reports to the Head of Technology Transformation who has responsibility for the delivery of Leap. They will bring experience delivering strategy across a complex organisation, including understanding its operational, cultural and governance impacts. You will be able to influence stakeholders, bring different perspectives together and align existing work with strategic priorities. You will have strong program management and reporting skills, and experience delivering a similar strategy or transformation program. Experience in higher education or a similarly complex industry is desirable. Who you are: Relevant tertiary qualifications with at least 10+ years’ experience leading and implementing large scale change initiatives in complex environments and/or extensive relevant experience gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Demonstrated experience using human-centred change principles in driving engagement, adoption and sustainable change in complex environments. Proven advanced program management experience, with an in-depth understanding of the project life cycle and strategic development of project plans, objectives, documentation and measurable outcomes Excellent consultation, influencing and negotiation skills and proven ability to engage effectively with diverse and senior stakeholders to build alignment, influence decision making and achieve successful outcomes. Extensive experience leading and influencing multi-disciplinary teams and delivery partners (architecture, data, engineering, cyber, business). Extensive experience establishing and managing program governance, reporting frameworks, risk management practices and quality assurance processes. Demonstrated strong analytical and problem-solving skills and proven capacity to exercise initiative, flexibility and to be proactive in development of robust solutions to problems. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Confidence using AI/Large language models. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply:  Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close:  Sunday November 1st at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
